(window.MAG=window.MAG||{}).d=(window.MAG.d||{});window.MAG.d["3-toledo-iv"]=[["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p1","","In the third year of the reign of our lord the most glorious prince Sisenand, on the Nones of December, in the era 681, while by the love of Christ and the diligence of the most religious King Sisenand of Spain and Gaul the priests at the city of Toledo in the name of the Lord had come together, that by his commands and orders admonished there might be treated by us concerning certain disciplines of the church a discussion, first to our Savior God almighty we gave thanks.","Anno tertio regnante domino nostro gloriosissimo principe Sisenando die Non. Dec. era DCLXXXI, dum studio amoris Christi ac diligentia religiosissimi Sisenandi regis Hispaniae atque Galliae sacerdotes apud Toletanam urbem in nomine domini convenissemus, ut eius imperiis atque iussis commoniti a nobis agitaretur de quibusdam ecclesiae disciplinis tractatus, primum gratias salvatori nostro deo omnipotenti egimus."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p2","","After these things, the afore-mentioned most excellent and glorious king, whose such great devotion toward God stands out that not only in human affairs but also in divine causes he remains solicitous—this one indeed, while in the basilica of the most blessed and holy martyr Leocadia the assembly of all of us together was present, on account of the merit of his faith with the most magnificent and most noble men having entered, first before the priests of God prostrate with tears and groans that for him intercession be made to the Lord requested, then with religious exhortation he exhorted the synod, that mindful of the paternal decrees to confirming among us the ecclesiastical rights we should apply study, and those things to correct which, while through negligence into use have come against ecclesiastical customs, for themselves the license from usurpation have made.","Post haec antefato ministro excellentissimo et glorioso regi, cuius tanta erga deum devotio exstat, ut non solum in rebus humanis, sed etiam in causis divinis sollicitus maneat. Hic quippe, dum in basilica beatissimae et sanctae martyris Leocadiae omnium nostrum pariter iam coetus adesset, pro merito fidei suae, cum magnificentissimis et nobilissimis viris ingressus, primum coram sacerdotibus dei humi prostratus cum lacrimis et gemitibus pro se interveniendum domino postulavit, deinde religiosa prosecutione synodum exhortatus est, ut paternorum decretorum memores ad confirmanda in nobis iura ecclesiastica studium praeberemus, et illa corrigere, quae, dum per neglegentiam in usum venerunt contra ecclesiasticos mores, licentiam sibi de usurpatione fecerunt."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p3","","Rejoicing therefore at his admonitions, it seemed necessary according to his and our vote to treat those things which are fitting, both in the divine sacraments, which in a diverse and illicit manner in the churches of Spain are celebrated, or those which in customs wickedly usurped are known. And since a general council we are holding, it is fitting that first the discourse of our voice be concerning God, that after the profession of faith the following vows of our work, as upon a most firm foundation, may be arranged.","Talibus igitur eius monitis congaudentes necessarium exstitit iuxta eius nostrumque votum tractare, quae competunt, sive in sacramentis divinis, quae diverso atque illicito modo in Spaniarum ecclesiis celebrantur, seu quae in moribus prave usurpata noscuntur. Et quoniam generale concilium agimus, oportet primum nostrae vocis sermonem de deo esse, ut post professionem fidei sequentia operis nostri vota, quasi super fundamentum firmissimum disponantur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p4","Confession of Faith","Just as from the holy fathers we have received, the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit of one deity and substance we confess, in the diversity of persons the Trinity believing, in the divinity unity preaching, neither persons do we confuse nor substance do we separate. The Father by no one made or begotten we say, the Son from the Father not made but begotten we assert, the Holy Spirit indeed neither created nor begotten, but proceeding from the Father and the Son we profess. Moreover the Lord Jesus Christ himself, the Son of God and creator of all things from the substance of the Father before the ages born, descended at the last time for the redemption of the world from the Father, who never ceases to be with the Father. For he was incarnate of the Holy Spirit and of the holy glorious mother of God, the virgin Mary, and born from her alone. The same Lord Jesus Christ, one in the holy Trinity, soul and flesh perfect without sin, receiving man, remaining that which he was, assuming that which he was not, equal to the Father according to divinity, less than the Father according to humanity, having in one person the property of two natures. For the natures in him are two, God and man, but not two sons and two gods, but the same one person in each nature; undergoing passion and death for our salvation, not in the power of divinity, but in the weakness of humanity, he descended to the lower regions, that the saints who were there held he might rescue, and having conquered the dominion of death he rose again, assumed into heaven, he is about to come in the future to the judgment of the living and of the dead, by whose death and blood washed we have obtained remission of sins, about to be raised by him on the last day in that flesh in which we now live, and in that form in which the same Lord rose again, about to receive from him some for the merits of justice eternal life, others for sins the sentence of eternal punishment. This is the faith of the catholic church, this confession we preserve and hold; which whoever most firmly shall have guarded, perpetual salvation he will have.","Quemadmodum a sanctis patribus accepimus, patrem et filium et spiritum sanctum unius deitatis atque substantiae confitemur, in personarum diversitate trinitatem credentes, in divinitate unitatem praedicantes nec personas confundimus nec substantiam separamus. Patrem a nullo factum vel genitum dicimus, filium a patre non factum, sed genitum asserimus, spiritum vero sanctum nec creatum nec genitum, sed procedentem ex patre et filio profitemur. Ipsum autem dominum Iesum Christum dei filium et creatorem omnium ex substantia patris ante saecula genitum descendisse ultimo tempore pro redemptione mundi a patre, qui numquam desinit esse cum patre, incarnatus est enim ex spiritu sancto et sancta gloriosa dei genetrice virgine Maria et natus ex ipsa solus. Idem dominus Iesus Christus unus in sancta trinitate anima et carne perfectus sine peccato suscipiens hominem manens, quod erat, assumens, quod non erat, aequalis patri secundum divinitatem, minor patre secundum humanitatem, habens in una persona duarum naturarum proprietatem, naturae enim in illo duae, deus et homo, non autem duo filii et dii duo, sed idem una persona in utraque natura; perferens passionem et mortem pro nostra salute, non in virtute divinitatis, sed infirmitate humanitatis, descendit ad inferos, ut sanctos, qui ibi tenebantur, erueret et devicto mortis imperio resurrexit, assumptus in caelis venturus est in futurum ad iudicium vivorum et mortuorum, cuius morte et sanguine mundati remissionem peccatorum consecuti sumus, resuscitandi ab eo in die novissima in ea, qua nunc vivimus, carne et in ea, qua resurrexit, idem dominus forma, percepturi ab ipso alii pro iustitiae meritis vitam aeternam, alii pro peccatis supplicii aeterni sententiam. Haec est catholicae ecclesiae fides, hanc confessionem conservamus atque tenemus, quam quisque firmissime custodierit, perpetuam salutem habebit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p5","Confession of Faith","I. After the right faith's confession, which in the holy church of God is preached, it pleased all the priests whom the unity of the catholic faith embraces, that nothing further diverse or dissonant in ecclesiastical sacraments we do, lest any our diversity among the ignorant or carnal the error of schism might seem to show and to many the variety of churches might stand for a scandal. One therefore the order of praying and of psalm-singing by us through all Spain and Gaul be preserved, one manner in the solemnities of masses, one in the evening offices, nor diverse be any longer among us ecclesiastical custom, because by one faith we are contained and kingdom. For this even the ancient canons decreed, that each province both of psalm-singing and of ministering a like custom hold.","Post rectae fidei confessionem, quae in sancta dei ecclesia praedicatur, placuit omnes sacerdotes, qui catholicae fidei unitate complectimur, ut nihil ultra diversum aut dissonum in ecclesiasticis sacramentis agamus, ne quaelibet nostra diversitas apud ignotos seu carnales schismatis errorem videatur ostendere et multis exstet in scandalum varietas ecclesiarum. Unus igitur ordo orandi atque psallendi a nobis per omnem Spaniam atque Galliam conservetur, unus modus in missarum sollemnitatibus, unus in vespertinis officiis, nec diversa sit ultra in nobis ecclesiastica consuetudo, quia una fide continemur et regno. Hoc enim et antiqui canones decreverunt, ut unaquaeque provincia et psallendi et ministrandi parem consuetudinem teneat."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p6","Confession of Faith","II. Almost no thing more the customs of discipline from the church of Christ has driven away than the negligence of priests, who, the canons having been despised, for correcting ecclesiastical customs a synod to make neglect. On account of this by us universally it has been defined, that because according to the ancient decrees of the fathers twice in the year the difficulty of time to have a council does not permit, at least once a year it be celebrated by us, yet in such a way that, if it is a matter of faith or any other common affair of the church, a general synod of all Spain and Gaul be convened; if truly neither concerning faith nor concerning the common utility of the church it shall be treated, it will be a special council, as the metropolitan of each province shall have chosen to be done. All moreover who causes against bishops or judges or powerful men or against any others they are known to have, to the same council let them run together, and whatever synodal examinations by anyone wickedly usurped are found, by the insistence of the royal executor most justly to those to whom the rights are, let them be restored, so that for compelling judges or secular men to the synod, by the study of the metropolitan an executor from the prince be requested. Moreover on the fifteenth day before the Kalends of June it is to be gathered in each province the synod on account of the spring season, when with herbs the earth is clothed and the food of shoots are found.","Nulla pene res disciplinae mores ab ecclesia Christi magis depulit, quam sacerdotum neglegentia, qui contemptis canonibus ad corrigendos ecclesiasticos mores synodum facere neglegunt. Ob hoc a nobis universaliter definitum est, ut, quia iuxta antiqua patrum decreta bis in anno difficultas temporis fieri concilium non sinit, saltem vel semel a nobis celebretur ita tamen, ut, si fidei causa est aut quaelibet alia ecclesiae communis, generalis totius Spaniae et Galliae synodus convocetur; si vero nec de fide, nec de communi ecclesiae utilitate tractabitur, speciale erit concilium, prout uniuscuiusque provinciae metropolitanus elegerit peragendum. Omnes autem, qui causas contra episcopos aut iudices ac potentes aut contra quoslibet alios habere noscuntur, ad eundem concilium concurrant, et quaecumque examina synodalia a quibuslibet prave usurpata inveniuntur regii exsecutoris instantia iustissime his, quibus iura sunt, reformentur, ita ut pro compellendis iudicibus vel saecularibus viris ad synodum metropolitani studio quidam exsecutor a principe postuletur. Quinta autem decima Kal. Iun. congreganda est in unaquaque provincia synodus propter vernale tempus, quando herbis terra vestitur et pabula germinum inveniuntur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p7","Confession of Faith","III. Therefore at the first hour of the day before the rising of the sun, let all be cast out from the church. And all doors having been shut, at one door through which the priests ought to enter, let the doorkeepers sit and all the bishops together let them enter, and according to the times of their ordination let them sit. After the entrance of all the bishops and the sitting, let there be called then the presbyters whom the cause shall have approved to enter. Let no one of the deacons thrust himself among them. After these let the deacons of probity enter, whom the order shall have demanded to be present, and a crown having been made from the seats of the bishops, let the presbyters behind them sit, let the deacons in the sight of the bishops stand. Then let the laity enter who by the choice of the council to be present have merited, then also the notaries whom for reciting or receiving the order requires, and let the doors be shut. And sitting in long silence the priests and heart only having toward God, let the archdeacon say: Pray. Immediately all on the ground shall prostrate themselves, and praying for a long time silently with tears and groans, one of the senior bishops rising let him pour forth a prayer to the Lord, with all still lying on the ground. The prayer moreover having been finished and the response by all \"Amen,\" again let the deacon say: Rise up; and quickly let all arise and with all fear of God and discipline, both bishops and presbyters let them sit, and thus with all in their places in silence sitting, let the deacon clothed with a white garment the book of canons into the midst bringing let him pronounce the chapters concerning councils to be held, and the titles having been finished, let the metropolitan bishop address the council saying: Behold, most holy priests, have been recited from the canons of the ancient fathers the sentences concerning a council to be celebrated. If therefore any action moves any one of you, before his brothers let him propose it.","Hora itaque diei prima ante solis ortum eiciantur omnes ab ecclesia. Obseratisque foribus cunctis ad unam ianuam, per quam sacerdotes ingredi oportet, ostiarii sedeant et convenientes omnes episcopi pariter introeant, et secundum ordinationis suae tempora resideant. Post ingressum omnium episcoporum atque consessum vocentur deinde presbyteri, quos causa probaverit introire. Nullus se inter eos ingerat diaconorum. Post hos ingrediantur diacones probabiles, quos ordo poposcerit interesse, et corona facta de sedibus episcoporum presbyteri a tergo eorum resideant, diacones in conspectu episcoporum stent. Deinde ingrediantur laici, qui electione concilii interesse meruerint, tum quoque et notarii, quos ad recitandum vel excipiendum ordo requirit, et obserentur ianuae. Sedentesque in diuturno silentio sacerdotes et cor tantum habentes ad deum, dicat archidiaconus: Orate. Statimque omnes in terram prostrabuntur, et orantes diutius tacite cum fletibus atque gemitibus, unus ex episcopis senioribus surgens orationem palam fundat ad dominum, cunctis adhuc in terra iacentibus. Finita autem oratione et responso ab omnibus Amen, rursus dicat diaconus: Erigite vos, et confestim omnes surgant et cum omni timore dei et disciplina, tam episcopi quam presbyteri sedeant sicque omnibus in suis locis in silentio consedentibus diaconus alba indutus codicem canonum in medio proferens capitula de conciliis agendis pronuntiet, finitisque titulis metropolitanus episcopus concilium alloquatur dicens: Ecce, sanctissimi sacerdotes, recitatae sunt ex canonibus priscorum patrum sententiae de concilio celebrando. Si qua igitur quempiam vestrum actio commovet coram suis fratribus proponat."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p8","Confession of Faith","Then if anyone whatever complaint which against the canon acts into the hearing of the priests shall have brought forward, not before to another chapter let it be passed, unless first that which has been proposed is terminated. For and if any presbyter or deacon or cleric or layman of those who outside shall have stood, the council on account of any matter to have been summoned shall have believed, to the archdeacon of the metropolitan church his cause let him make known, and he let him announce to the council. Then to him both of entering and of proposing let license be granted. Let no one moreover of the bishops from the common assembly depart before the hour of general departure arrives. The council also let no one dare to dissolve, unless all things shall have been determined, so that whatever by common deliberation are finished, by the hands of the individual bishops be subscribed. For then God among his priests to be present is to be believed, if with all tumult having been cast aside solicitously and tranquilly the ecclesiastical affairs are terminated.","Tunc si aliquis quamcumque querelam, quae contra canonem agit, in audientiam sacerdotalem protulerit, non prius ad aliud transeatur capitulum, nisi primum, quae praeposita est actio, terminetur. Nam et si presbyter aliquis aut diaconus vel clericus sive laicus de his, qui foris steterint, concilium pro qualibet re crediderit appellandum, ecclesiae metropolitanae archidiacono causam suam intimet, et ille concilio denuntiet. Tunc illi et introeundi et proponendi licentia concedatur. Nullus autem episcoporum a coetu communi secedat antequam hora generalis secessionis adveniat. Concilium quoque nullus solvere audeat, nisi fuerint cuncta determinata, ita ut quaecumque deliberatione communi finiuntur, episcoporum singulorum manibus subscribantur. Tunc enim deus suorum sacerdotum interesse credendus est, si tumultu omni abiecto sollicite atque tranquille ecclesiastica negotia terminentur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p9","Confession of Faith","IIII. It is customary in Spain concerning the solemnity of Easter a variety of preaching to exist. A diverse observation indeed of calendars of the paschal festivity sometimes error brings forth. Therefore it pleased, that before three months of the Epiphany the metropolitan priests by letters one another ask, that with common knowledge taught the day of the resurrection of Christ to their fellow-provincials they might make known and at one time to be celebrated they announce.","Solet in Spaniis de solemnitate paschali varietas exsistere praedicationis. Diversa enim observantia laterculorum paschalis festivitatis interdum errorem parturit. Proinde placuit, ut ante tres menses epiphaniorum metropolitani sacerdotes litteris se invicem inquirant, ut communi scientia docti diem resurrectionis Christi comprovincialibus suis insinuent et uno tempore celebrandum annuntient."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p10","Confession of Faith","V. Concerning the sacrament of baptism indeed, on account of which in Spain some priests a triple, some a single immersion make, by not a few a schism is seen and the unity of faith is seen to be cut. For while diverse parties in baptizing in a contrary manner do something, by others not to have been baptized they contend. Therefore what by us in the diversity of the sacrament is to be done, let us be informed by the precepts of the apostolic see, not our own but the paternal institution following. Gregory therefore of blessed memory, the pontiff of the Roman church, who not only the parts of Italy illustrated but also far existing churches by his doctrine fully taught, the most holy Leander bishop earnestly asking concerning this diversity of Spain what rather ought to be followed, among other things writing back to him thus says: \"Concerning the triple immersion of baptism nothing can be answered more truly than you yourselves have perceived, because in one faith nothing effects the diverse custom of the holy church. We indeed, that we immerse a third time, the sacraments of the three-day burial we signify, that while a third time from the waters the infant is brought forth, the resurrection of three-day time may be expressed. But if anyone perhaps even for the veneration of the highest Trinity thinks it to be done, nor to this does anything oppose, the one to be baptized once into the water to immerse, because while in three subsistence one substance is, reprehensible it could never be, the infant in baptism into water either three times or once to immerse, when both in three immersions the Trinity of persons and in one the singularity of divinity can be designated. But if hitherto by heretics the infant in baptism a third time was immersed, to be done among you I do not think, lest while the immersions they number, the divinity they divide, and while that which they were doing they do, your custom them to have conquered they might boast. On which account because concerning each sacrament which is done in holy baptism by so great a man an account has been given, that each right, each irreprehensible in the holy church of God be held, for avoiding however the scandal of schism or the use of heretical dogma, let us hold the single immersion of baptism, lest they be seen among us who a third time immerse the assertion of heretics to approve, while they follow both the custom, and lest perhaps to anyone might be doubtful the mystery of this single sacrament, let him see in it the death and resurrection of Christ to be signified. For in the waters immersion as into hell a descent is, and again from the waters an emersion a resurrection is. Likewise let them see in it the unity of divinity and the Trinity of persons to be shown: the unity, while once we immerse; the Trinity, while in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit we baptize. It is unfolded of this singular baptism the mystery also by the examples of the holy Scriptures, the apostle Paul testifying: I do not wish you to be ignorant, brothers, that our fathers all under the cloud were and all the sea crossed and all in Moses were baptized in the cloud and in the sea. Moreover the Red Sea signifies baptism consecrated by the blood of Christ, through which the people of God once passed across, where nevertheless the whole Trinity was, the column of fire and of cloud preceding the people. In fire indeed the Father is signified, in the column the Son, in the cloud the Holy Spirit. The Jordan also's flowing with the ark the people of God once crossed across, through which the single immersion of baptism is signified, by whose sacrament the church is washed and from the labors of this world through baptism as through the Jordan into the land of heavenly promise it enters.\"","De baptismi autem sacramento, propter quod in Spaniis quidam sacerdotes trinam, simplam quidam mersionem faciunt, a nonnullis schisma esse conspicitur et unitas fidei scindi videtur. Nam dum partes diversae in baptizandis aliqua contrario modo agunt, ab aliis non baptizatos esse contendunt. Proinde quid a nobis in sacramenti diversitate fiendum sit, apostolicae sedis informemur praeceptis non nostram, sed paternam institutionem sequentes. Beatae igitur memoriae Gregorius Romanae ecclesiae pontifex, qui non solum partes Italiae illustravit, sed et longe exsistentes ecclesias sua doctrina perdocuit, efflagitante sanctissimo Leandro episcopo de hac Spaniae diversitate, quid potius esset sequendum, inter cetera rescribens ei sic ait: De trina vero mersione baptismatis nihil responderi verius potest, quam ipsi sensistis, quia in una fide nihil efficit sanctae ecclesiae consuetudo diversa. Nos autem, quod tertio mergimus, triduanae sepulturae sacramenta signamus, ut, dum tertio ab aquis infans educitur, resurrectio triduani temporis exprimatur. Quod si quis forte etiam pro summae trinitatis veneratione existimet fieri, neque ad hoc aliquid obsistit baptizandum semel in aquas mergere, quia dum in tribus subsistentiis una substantia est, repraehensibile esse nullatenus poterit infantem in baptismate in aquam vel ter vel semel mergere, quando et in tribus mersionibus personarum trinitas et in una potest divinitatis singularitas designari. Sed si hucusque ab hereticis infans in baptismate tertio mergebatur, fiendum apud vos esse non censeo, ne dum mersiones numerant, divinitatem dividant, dumque, quod faciebant, faciunt, morem vestrum se vicisse glorientur. Quapropter quia de utroque sacramento, quod fit in sancto baptismo a tanto viro reddita est ratio, quod utrumque rectum, utrumque irrepraehensibile in sancta dei ecclesia habeatur, propter vitandum autem schismatis scandalum vel heretici dogmatis usum simplam teneamus baptismi mersionem, ne videantur apud nos, qui tertio mergunt, hereticorum approbare assertionem, dum sequuntur et morem, et ne forte cuiquam sit dubium huius simpli mysterium sacramenti, videat in eo mortem et resurrectionem Christi significari. Nam in aquis mersio quasi in infernum descensio est et rursus ab aquis emersio resurrectio est. Item videant in eo unitatem divinitatis et trinitatem personarum ostendi: unitatem, dum semel mergimus; trinitatem, dum in nomine patris et filii et spiritus sancti baptizamus. Panditur huius singularis baptismatis mysterium etiam scripturarum sanctarum exemplis Paulo apostolo attestante: Nolo vos ignorare, fratres, quoniam patres nostri omnes sub nube fuerunt et omnes mare transierunt et omnes in Moyse baptizati sunt in nube et in mari. Mare quippe rubrum significat baptismum Christi sanguine consecratum, per quod populus dei semel transiit, ubi tamen tota erat trinitas praecedente populum columna ignis et nubis. In igne quippe significatur pater, in columna filius, in nube spiritus sanctus. Iordanis quoque fluenta cum arca populus dei semel transiit, per quod significatur simpla mersio baptismatis, cuius sacramento ecclesia abluitur et de saeculi huius laboribus per baptismum quasi per Iordanem ad terram caelestis repromissionis ingreditur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p11","Confession of Faith","VI. We have found that through not a few churches on the day of the sixth feria of the Lord's passion, with the doors of the basilicas shut, neither is the office celebrated nor the passion of the Lord to the peoples is preached, while the same Savior our Lord to his apostles commands saying: My passion and death and resurrection to all preach. Therefore it is fitting on the same day the mystery of the cross, which the Lord himself to all to be announced willed, to be preached, and the indulgence of crimes with clear voice all the people to ask, that by the compunction of penance cleansed the venerable day of the Lord's resurrection with iniquities having been remitted we may merit to receive, and of his body and blood the sacrament, cleansed from sin, we may take.","Comperimus, quod per nonnullas ecclesias in die sextae feriae passionis domini clausis basilicarum foribus nec celebretur officium nec passio domini populis praedicetur, dum idem salvator noster apostolis suis praecipiat dicens: Passionem et mortem et resurrectionem meam omnibus praedicate. Ideoque oportet eodem die mysterium crucis, quod ipse dominus cunctis annuntiandum voluit, praedicari atque indulgentiam criminum clara voce omnem populum postulare, ut poenitentiae compunctione mundati venerabilem diem dominicae resurrectionis remissis iniquitatibus suscipere mereamur corporisque eius et sanguinis sacramentum mundi a peccato sumamus."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p12","Confession of Faith","VII. Some on the day of the same Lord's passion from the ninth hour break the fast, to banquets are given, and while the sun itself on the same day with darkness grew pale, the light withdrew, and the elements themselves troubled the sadness of the whole world showed, they the fast of so great a day pollute and to feasts attend. And because the whole same day the universal church on account of the passion of the Lord in sorrow and abstinence passes, whoever on it the fast besides infants, old men and the infirm, before the completed prayers of indulgence shall have broken, from the paschal joy let him be driven, nor in it the sacrament of the body and blood of the Lord let him receive, who the day of his passion through abstinence does not honor.","Quidam in die eiusdem dominicae passionis ab hora nona ieiunium solvunt, conviviis adhibentur, et dum sol ipse eadem die tenebris palluerit, lumen subduxerit, ipsaque elementa turbata mestitiam totius mundi ostenderint, illi ieiunium tanti diei polluunt epulisque inserviunt, et quia totum eundem diem universalis ecclesia propter passionem domini in merore et abstinentia peragit, quicumque in eo ieiunium praeter parvulos, senes et languidos ante peractas indulgentiae preces solverit, a paschali gaudio depellatur, nec in eo sacramentum corporis et sanguinis domini percipiat, qui diem passionis eius per abstinentiam non honorat."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p13","Confession of Faith","VIII. The lantern and the candle within the vigils in some churches are not blessed, and why by us they are blessed they ask. On account of the glorious mystery of the night itself solemnly these things we bless, that the sacred resurrection of Christ the mystery, which at the time of this votive night arrives, by the blessing of sanctified light we may receive, and because this observance through the places of many lands and regions of Spain in churches is commended, it is fitting that on account of the unity of peace in the Gallican churches it be preserved. To no one moreover with impunity shall it be, who these things shall have despised, but to the rules of the fathers let him be subject.","Lucerna et cereus infra vigilias apud quasdam ecclesias non benedicitur et, cur a nobis benedicatur, inquirunt. Propter gloriosum enim noctis ipsius sacramentum sollemniter haec benedicimus, ut sacrae resurrectionis Christi mysterium, quod tempore huius votivae noctis advenit, benedictione sanctificati luminis suscipiamus, et quia haec observatio per multarum loca terrarum regionesque Spaniae in ecclesiis commendatur, dignum est, ut propter unitatem pacis in Gallicanis ecclesiis conservetur. Nulli autem impune erit, qui haec contempserit, sed patrum regulis subiacebit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p14","Confession of Faith","VIIII. Not a few priests in Spain are found who the Lord's prayer, which our Savior taught and commanded, not daily, but only on the Lord's day say. And because without intermission we ought to pray, as the apostle taught, how moreover we ought to pray, Christ commanded saying: When you pray, say: Our Father who art in heaven. How therefore daily is it not said, what without intermission to be said is commanded? For in so much daily this prayer is to be said, that even by the very title it is used, for it is called the daily prayer. Thus the holy fathers named it, which also among the doctors, whose illustrious doctrine is, is found. The holy Cyprian indeed says: Therefore in the Lord's prayer we ask that our bread, that is Christ, be given to us daily, that we who in Christ remain and live, from his sanctification and body not depart. The holy Hilary says: Our daily bread give us today. For what does God so much will as that daily Christ dwell in us, who is the bread of life and the bread from heaven? And because it is a daily prayer, daily also that it be given is prayed? The holy Augustine says: For daily and brief sins, without which this life is not led, the daily prayer of the faithful makes satisfaction. For it is of them to say: Our Father, who art in heaven, who already to the Father by such regeneration have been born again of water and the Holy Spirit. Therefore this daily prayer blots out the smallest daily sins, it blots out those from which the life of the faithful even wickedly done by repenting changed for the better departs. Therefore as Christ commanded, as the apostle admonished, and as the ecclesiastical doctors established, because daily either in thought or in word we sin, daily this prayer into the sight of God we ought to pour forth. Whoever therefore of the priests or of the subject clerics this Lord's prayer daily either in the public or in the private office shall have passed over on account of pride, let him be judged and of the honor of his order let him be deprived.","Nonnulli sacerdotum in Spaniis reperiuntur, qui dominicam orationem, quam salvator noster docuit et praecepit, non cotidie, sed tantum die dominica dicunt, et quia sine intermissione oremus, ut apostolus docuit, qualiter autem oremus, Christus praecepit dicens: Cum autem oratis, dicite: Pater noster qui es in caelis. Quomodo ergo cotidie non dicitur, quod sine intermissione dici iubetur? Nam in tantum cotidie haec oratio dicenda est, ut etiam ipso titulo utatur, nam vocatur oratio cotidiana. Sic eam sancti patres nuncupaverunt, quod etiam apud doctores, quorum illustris doctrina est, invenitur. Sanctus quippe Cyprianus dicit: Itaque in oratione dominica panem nostrum, id est Christum, dari nobis cotidie petimus, ut, qui in Christo manemus et vivimus, a sanctificatione et corpore eius non recedamus. Sanctus Hilarius dicit: Panem nostrum cotidianum da nobis hodie. Quid enim tam vult deus quam, ut cotidie Christus habitet in nobis, qui est panis vitae et panis de caelo? Et quia cotidiana oratio est, cotidie quoque, ut detur, oratur? Sanctus Augustinus dicit: De cotidianis autem brevibusque peccatis, sine quibus vita haec non ducitur, cotidiana oratio fidelium satisfacit. Eorum est enim dicere: Pater noster, qui es in caelis, qui iam patri tali regenerati sunt ex aqua et spiritu sancto. Delet igitur haec cotidiana oratio minima cotidiana peccata, delet illa, a quibus vita fidelium etiam scelerate gesta poenitendo in melius mutata discedit. Ergo sicut Christus praecepit, sicut apostolus admonuit, et quemadmodum doctores ecclesiastici instituerunt, quia cotidie vel cogitatione vel verbo delinquimus, cotidie hanc orationem effundere in conspectu dei debemus. Quisquis ergo sacerdotum vel subiacentium clericorum hanc orationem dominicam cotidie aut in publico aut in privato officio praeterierit propter superbiam, iudicatus ordinis sui honore multetur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p15","Confession of Faith","X. Likewise we have known that some priests of Spain in the days of Lent \"alleluia\" sing, except in the last week of Easter, which from now on to be done we forbid, establishing that in all the aforesaid days of Lent, because it is the time not of joy but of sorrow, \"alleluia\" not be sung. For then it is fitting to tears and fastings to attend, the body with sackcloth and ashes to clothe, the mind with sorrow to cast down, joy into sadness to turn, until the time of the resurrection of Christ shall come, when it is fitting already in gladness to remain and sorrow into joy to change. For this the universal consent of the church in all parts of the earth has strengthened, which also by all of us to be preserved through the provinces of Spain and Gaul will be fitting. In the remaining times also, that is the Kalends of January, which on account of the error of gentility are observed, altogether \"alleluia\" shall not be sung, in which also besides fish and vegetables, as also in those forty days, from other meats it is abstained, and by some also even wine is not drunk. If anyone therefore bishop or presbyter or deacon or any from the order of clerics shall have been found who his own judgment to this constitution thinks to be preferred, let him be compelled to be deprived of the office of his order and of the communion of the same Easter let him be deprived.","Item cognovimus quosdam Spaniae sacerdotes, qui in quadragesimae diebus alleluia decantent praeter in ultima hebdomada paschae, quod deinceps fieri interdicimus statuentes, ut in omnibus praedictis quadragesimae diebus, quia tempus est non gaudii, sed meroris, alleluia non decantetur. Tunc enim opus est fletibus ac ieiuniis insistere, corpus cilicio et cinere induere, animum meroribus deicere, gaudium in tristitiam vertere, quousque veniet tempus resurrectionis Christi, quando oporteat iam in laetitia manere et merorem in gaudium commutare. Hoc enim ecclesiae universalis consensio in cunctis terrarum partibus roboravit, quod et a nobis omnibus, ut conservetur per Spanias Galliasque provincias oportebit. In temporibus quoque reliquis, id est Kalend. Ianuar., quae propter errorem gentilitatis aguntur, omnino alleluia non decantabitur, in quibus etiam praeter piscem et olus, sicut et in illis XL diebus ceteris carnibus abstinetur et a quibusdam etiam nec vinum bibitur. Si quis igitur episcopus aut presbyter aut diaconus aut quilibet ex ordine clericorum fuerit repertus, qui arbitrium suum huic constitutioni aestimet praeferendum, ordinis sui officio carere cogatur et communione eiusdem paschae privetur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p16","Confession of Faith","XI. In some also of the churches of Spain the \"laudes\" after the apostle are sung, before the gospel is preached, while the canons command after the apostle not \"laudes\" but the gospel to announce. For it is a presumption that they place before those things which ought to follow. For the \"laudes\" therefore follow the gospel on account of the glory of Christ, which through the same gospel is preached. Concerning all therefore priests let this order from now on be retained, about to receive the penalty of excommunication, who this order shall have disturbed.","In quibusdam quoque Spaniarum ecclesiis laudes post apostolum decantantur, priusquam evangelium praedicetur, dum canones praecipiant post apostolum non laudes, sed evangelium annuntiare, praesumptio est enim, ut anteponant ea, quae sequi debent. Nam laudes ideo evangelium sequuntur propter gloriam Christi, quae per idem evangelium praedicatur. Circa omnes igitur sacerdotes hic ordo deinceps retineatur, excommunicationis poenam suscepturi, qui hunc ordinem perturbaverint."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p17","Confession of Faith","XII. Concerning hymns also to be sung both of the Savior and of the apostles we have an example. For both the Lord himself is reported to have said a hymn, Matthew the evangelist testifying: And a hymn having been said, they went out into the Mount of Olives. And Paul the apostle to the Ephesians wrote saying: Be filled with the Spirit, speaking to yourselves in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs. And because by some hymns by human study in the praise of God and of the apostles and martyrs the triumphs composed are known, just as those which the most blessed doctors Hilary and Ambrose published, which however some specifically reject on this account, that from the writings of the holy canons or apostolic tradition they do not exist. Let them therefore reject also that hymn by men composed, which daily in the public and private office at the end of all psalms we say: \"Glory and honor to the Father and to the Son and to the Holy Spirit unto ages of ages. Amen.\" For also that hymn which at the birth of Christ in the flesh the angels sang: \"Glory to God in the highest and on earth peace to men of good will,\" the rest which there follow, the ecclesiastical doctors composed. Therefore not even those in churches are to be sung, because in the books of the holy Scriptures they are not found? Masses are composed or prayers or orations or commendations or impositions of hands, from which, if nothing be sung in the church, all ecclesiastical offices are vacant. The apostle admonishes these things to be done and exhorts Timothy saying: I urge therefore first of all that supplications, prayers, intercessions, thanksgivings be made for all men, for kings and for all who are in high positions. Therefore as prayers, so also hymns in the praise of God composed, let no one of us further disapprove, but in like manner Gaul and Spain celebrate. By excommunication to be punished, who hymns to reject shall have been dared.","De hymnis etiam canendis et salvatoris et apostolorum habemus exemplum. Nam et ipse dominus hymnum dixisse perhibetur Matthaeo evangelista testante: Et hymno dicto exierunt in montem Oliveti. Et Paulus apostolus ad Ephesios scripsit dicens: Implemini spiritu loquentes vobis in psalmis et hymnis et canticis spiritalibus. Et quia a nonnullis hymni humano studio in laudem dei atque apostolorum et martyrum triumphos compositi esse noscuntur, sicut hi, quos beatissimi doctores Hilarius atque Ambrosius ediderunt, quos tamen quidam specialiter reprobant pro eo, quod de scripturis sanctorum canonum vel apostolica traditione non exsistunt. Respuant ergo et illum hymnum ab hominibus compositum, quem cotidie publico privatoque officio in fine omnium psalmorum dicimus: Gloria et honor patri et filio et spiritui sancto in saecula saeculorum. Amen. Nam et ille hymnus, quem nato in carne Christo angeli cecinerunt: Gloria in excelsis deo et in terra pax hominibus bonae voluntatis, reliqua, quae ibi sequuntur, ecclesiastici doctores composuerunt. Ergo nec ipsi in ecclesiis canendi sunt, quia in sanctarum scripturarum libris non inveniuntur? Componuntur missae sive preces vel orationes sive commendationes seu manus impositiones, ex quibus, si nulla decantetur in ecclesia, vacant officia omnia ecclesiastica. Admonet haec fieri atque hortatur Timotheum apostolus dicens: Obsecro igitur primo omnium fieri obsecrationes, orationes, postulationes, gratiarum actiones pro omnibus hominibus, pro regibus et pro omnibus, qui in sublimitatibus sunt. Sicut igitur orationes, ita et hymnos in laudem dei compositos nullus nostrum ulterius improbet, sed pari modo Gallia Spaniaque celebret. Excommunicatione plectendi, qui hymnos reicere fuerint ausi."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p18","Confession of Faith","XIII. The hymn also of the three children, in which the whole creature of heaven and earth praises God and which the catholic church through the whole world diffused celebrates, some priests in the mass of the Lord's days and in the solemnities of the martyrs to sing neglect. Therefore the holy council has established, that through all the churches of Spain and Gaul in the solemnity of all masses the same in public be sung, about to lose communion, who the ancient custom of this hymn and our definition shall have exceeded.","Hymnum quoque trium puerorum, in quo universa caeli terraeque creatura deum collaudat et quem ecclesia catholica per totum orbem diffusa celebrat, quidam sacerdotes in missa dominicorum dierum et in sollemnitatibus martyrum canere neglegunt. Proinde sanctum concilium instituit, ut per omnes ecclesias Spaniae vel Galliae in omnium missarum solemnitate idem in publico decantetur, communionem amissuri, qui antiquam huius hymni consuetudinem nostramque definitionem excesserint."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p19","Confession of Faith","XIIII. At the end of the psalms not, as by some hitherto, \"Glory to the Father,\" but \"Glory and honor to the Father\" be said, David the prophet teaching: Give to the Lord glory and honor, and John the evangelist of the army saying: Honor and glory to our God sitting on the throne. And through this these two thus it is fitting on earth to be said, as in heaven they resound. To all therefore ecclesiastics this observance we give, which whoever shall have passed over, the loss of communion he will have.","In fine psalmorum non, sicut a quibusdam hucusque, gloria patri, sed gloria et honor patri dicatur David propheta docente: Afferte domino gloriam et honorem, et Ioanne evangelista exercitus dicentium: Honor et gloria deo nostro sedenti in throno. Ac per hoc haec duo sic oportet in terris dici, sicut in caelis resonant. Universis igitur ecclesiasticis hanc observantiam damus, quam, quisque praeterierit, communionis iacturam habebit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p20","Confession of Faith","XV. There are some who at the end of responsories the \"glory\" do not say, on which account sometimes inconveniently it resounds, but this is the distinction, that in joyful things let the \"glory\" follow, in sadder things let the beginning be repeated.","Sunt quidam, qui in fine responsoriorum gloriam non dicunt, propter quod interdum inconvenienter resonat, sed haec est discretio, ut in laetis sequatur gloria, in tristioribus repetatur principium."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p21","Confession of Faith","XVI. The book of the Apocalypse the authority of many councils and the synodical decrees of the holy Roman prelates write to be of John the evangelist, and among the divine books to be received they established. And because very many are who its authority do not receive and it in the church of God to preach despise, if anyone it from now on either shall not have received or from Easter until Pentecost at the time of the masses in the church shall not have preached, the sentence of excommunication he will have.","Apocalypsin librum multorum conciliorum auctoritas et synodica sanctorum praesulum Romanorum decreta Ioannis evangelistae esse rescribunt, et inter divinos libros recipiendum constituerunt. Et quia plurimi sunt, qui eius auctoritatem non recipiunt eamque in ecclesia dei praedicare contemnunt, si quis eum deinceps aut non receperit aut a pascha usque ad pentecosten missarum tempore in ecclesia non praedicaverit, excommunicationis sententiam habebit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p22","Confession of Faith","XVII. Not a few priests after the Lord's prayer having been said immediately communicate and afterwards the blessing on the people give, which from now on we forbid, but after the Lord's prayer let the blessing upon the people follow, and then at length the sacrament of the body and blood of the Lord be taken, by that order clearly, that the priest and the levite before the altar communicate, in the choir the clergy, outside the choir the people.","Nonnulli sacerdotes post dictam orationem dominicam statim communicant et postea benedictionem in populo dant, quod deinceps interdicimus, sed post orationem dominicam benedictio in populum sequatur, et tunc demum corporis et sanguinis domini sacramentum sumatur, eo videlicet ordine, ut sacerdos et levita ante altare communicent, in choro clerus, extra chorum populus."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p23","Confession of Faith","XVIII. A pernicious custom is by no means to be received, which passing over the statutes of the elders the whole order of the church has disturbed, while some by ambition seek priesthoods, others by offered gifts assume the pontificate, not a few also with crimes implicated or to secular military service given, unworthy to the honor of the highest and sacred order have come, concerning whose cause indeed and removal it would have been fitting to establish, but lest a very great disturbance of the church might arise. With the past omitted, for the future, who are not to be promoted to the priesthood according to the rules of the canons, we think it necessary to insert: that is, who in any crime have been detected, who with the mark of infamy have been sprinkled, who any crimes through public penance to have committed have confessed, who into heresy have lapsed, who in heresy baptized or rebaptized are known to be, who have mutilated themselves or naturally by defect of members or by cutting off anything less to have are known, who the union of a second wife have obtained or numerous marriages have frequented, who a widow or from a husband left have married or of corrupted husbands have been, who concubines or fornicatresses have had, who to servile condition are liable, who are unknown, who are neophytes or laymen, who to secular military service are given, who to the curia's bonds are obliged, who ignorant of letters are, who not yet to thirty years have come, who through ecclesiastical grades have not advanced, who by ambition seek honor, who by gifts to obtain honor strive, who by predecessors in the priesthood are chosen, but neither shall he from now on be a priest whom neither the clergy nor the people of his own city has chosen nor the authority of the metropolitan or the assent of the fellow-provincial priests has sought.","Perniciosa consuetudo nequaquam est recipienda, quae maiorum statuta praeteriens omnem ecclesiae ordinem perturbavit, dum alii per ambitus sacerdotia appetunt, alii oblatis muneribus pontificatum assumunt, nonnulli etiam sceleribus implicati vel saeculari militiae dediti indigni ad honorem summi ac sacri ordinis pervenerunt, de quorum scilicet causa atque remotione oportuerat quidem statuere, sed ne perturbatio quamplurima ecclesiae oriretur. Praeteritis omissis deinceps, qui non promoveantur ad sacerdotium ex regulis canonum, necessario credimus inserendum, id est, qui in aliquo crimine detecti sunt, qui infamiae nota aspersi sunt, qui scelera aliqua per publicam poenitentiam admisisse confessi sunt, qui in heresim lapsi sunt, qui in heresi baptizati aut rebaptizati esse noscuntur, qui semetipsos absciderunt aut naturaliter defectu membrorum decisione aliquid minus habere noscuntur, qui secundae uxoris coniunctionem sortiti sunt aut numerosa coniugia frequentaverunt, qui viduam vel a marito relictam duxerunt aut corruptarum mariti fuerunt, qui concubinas aut fornicarias habuerunt, qui servili condicione obnoxii sunt, qui ignoti sunt, qui neophyti vel laici sunt, qui saeculari militiae dediti sunt, qui curiae nexibus obligati sunt, qui inscii litterarum sunt, qui nondum ad triginta annos pervenerunt, qui per gradus ecclesiasticos non accesserunt, qui ambitu honorem quaerunt, qui muneribus honorem obtinere moliuntur, qui a decessoribus in sacerdotium eleguntur, sed nec ille deinceps sacerdos erit, quem nec clerus nec populus propriae civitatis elegit vel auctoritas metropolitani vel comprovincialium sacerdotum assensio exquisivit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p24","Confession of Faith","Whoever therefore from now on to the order of the priesthood sought and in those things which are predicted sought and in none of these things shall have been detected and examined of probable life and doctrine shall have been, then according to the synodal or decretal constitutions with the will of all the clerics or citizens by all the fellow-provincial bishops or certainly by three in the priesthood on the Lord's day he shall be consecrated, the others who are absent concurring by their letters, and rather by the authority or presence of him who is in the metropolis constituted. A bishop moreover fellow-provincial there is to be consecrated where the metropolitan shall have chosen, a metropolitan moreover not unless in the city of the metropolis, his fellow-provincials there together convening. If anyone moreover from now on against the predicted forbidden of the canons to the grade of priesthood unworthy to aspire shall have contended, with his ordainers of the obtained honor the peril let him undergo.","Quicumque igitur deinceps ad ordinem sacerdotii postulatus et in his, quae praedicta sunt, exquisitus in nullo horum depraehensus fuerit et examinatus probabili vita atque doctrina exstiterit, tunc secundum synodalia vel decretalia constituta cum omnium clericorum vel civium voluntate ab universis comprovincialibus episcopis aut certe a tribus in sacerdotum die dominica consecrabitur, conhibentibus ceteris, qui absentes sunt litteris suis, et magis auctoritate vel praesentia eius, qui est in metropoli constitutus. Episcopus autem comprovincialis ibi consecrandus est, ubi metropolitanus elegerit, metropolitanus autem non nisi in civitate metropoli comprovincialibus ibidem convenientibus. Si quis autem deinceps contra praedicta vetita canonum ad gradum sacerdotum indignus aspirare contenderit, cum ordinatoribus suis adepti honoris periculo subiacebit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p25","Confession of Faith","XVIIII. In the old law from the twenty-fifth year the levites in the tabernacle to serve are commanded, whose authority in the canons also the holy fathers followed. We both of the divine law and of the councils the precept unmindful, infants and boys we make levites before the legitimate age, before the experience of life. Therefore lest further it be done, by both the divine law and the canons we admonish by the sentences, but at twenty-five years of age let the levites be consecrated and at thirty let presbyters be ordained, so that according to the precept let them first be proved, and thus let them minister, having no crime.","In veteri lege ab anno vigesimo et quinto levitae in tabernaculo servire mandantur, cuius auctoritatem in canonibus et sancti patres secuti sunt. Nos et divinae legis et conciliorum praecepti immemores infantes et pueros levitas facimus ante legitimam aetatem, ante experientiam vitae. Ideoque ne ulterius fiat, a nobis et divinae legis et canonum admonemus sententiis, sed viginti quinque annorum aetatis levitae consecrentur et triginta presbyteri ordinentur, ita ut secundum praeceptum probentur primum, et sic ministrent nullum crimen habentes."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p26","Confession of Faith","XX. Whoever in the priesthood of God are placed, irreprehensible they ought to be, Paul the apostle testifying: It behooves a bishop to be irreprehensible. Inoffensive therefore and immaculate it befits the priests of God to exist, nor by any contagion of fornication let them be polluted, but chastely living, pure themselves for celebrating the sacraments let them present. Let us abstain therefore ourselves from every evil work, and from every defilement of the flesh free let us remain, that pure in body, cleansed in mind we may be able to the sacrifice of Christ worthy to approach and God for the offenses of all to beseech.","Quicumque in sacerdotio dei positi sunt, irrepraehensibiles esse debent Paulo apostolo attestante: Oportet episcopum irrepraehensibilem esse. Inoffensos igitur et immaculatos decet dei exsistere sacerdotes, nec ullius eos fornicationis contagio pollui, sed caste viventes mundos semetipsos celebrandis exhibeant sacramentis. Abstineamus ergo nos ab omni opere malo, et ab omni inquinamento carnis liberi maneamus, ut mundi corpore, purgati mente possimus ad sacrificium Christi digni accedere et deum pro delictis omnium deprecari."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p27","Confession of Faith","XXI. Although a pure conscience before God us to have it behooves, yet also among men an excellent fame to guard it is fitting, that according to the apostolic precept not only before God but also before men of holy life testimony we may have. For some hitherto of the priests no small scandal have created, while in the conversation of life not of good fame they exist. That therefore from now on every wicked occasion of suspicion may be excluded, and lest there be given further to seculars a place of detracting, it behooves bishops to have the testimony of probable persons of conversation and of life in their chamber, that they may please God by good conversation and the church by excellent fame.","Quamvis conscientiam puram apud deum nos habere oporteat, tamen et apud homines famam optimam custodire convenit, ut iuxta praeceptum apostolicum non tantum coram deo, sed etiam coram hominibus vitae sanctae testimonium habeamus. Quidam enim hucusque sacerdotum non modicum scandalum creaverunt, dum in conversatione vitae non bonae famae exsistunt. Ut igitur excludatur deinceps omnis nefanda suspicionis occasio et, ne detur ultra saecularibus obtrectandi locus, oportet episcopos testimonium probabilium personarum conversationis et vitae in conclavi suo habere, ut deo placeant per conversationem bonam et ecclesiae per optimam famam."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p28","Confession of Faith","XXII. Not otherwise it has pleased, that as the prelates, so also the presbyters and the levites, whom perhaps infirmity or the gravity of age in the chamber to remain does not permit, that the same in their cells witnesses of life may have and their life as in name so also in merits they may hold.","Non aliter placuit, ut quemadmodum antistites, ita presbyteri atque levitae, quos forte infirmitas aut aetatis gravitas in conclavi manere non sinit, ut eidem in cellulis suis testes vitae habeant vitamque suam sicut nomine ita et meritis teneant."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p29","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXIII. Prone is every age from adolescence into evil; for nothing is more uncertain than the life of adolescents. On this account it was fitting to be established, that if any in the clergy boys or adolescents exist, all in one chamber of the court let them abide, that the slippery years of age not in luxury, but in ecclesiastical disciplines they may spend, assigned to a most approved elder, whom both a teacher of discipline and a witness of life they may have. But if any among these orphans exist, let them be fostered by priestly guardianship, that both their life may be from crimes untouched and their property from the injury of the wicked. Who moreover to these precepts shall have resisted, let them be assigned to monasteries, that wandering and proud minds by a more severe rule may be constrained.","Prona est omnis aetas ab adolescentia in malum, nihil enim incertius quam vita adolescentium. Ob hoc constituendum oportuit, ut, si qui in clero pueri aut adolescentes exsistunt, omnes in una conclavi atrii commorentur, ut lubricae aetatis annos non in luxuria, sed in disciplinis ecclesiasticis agant deputati probatissimo seniori, quem et magistrum doctrinae et testem vitae habeant. Quod si aliqui ex his pupilli exsistunt, sacerdotali tutela foveantur, ut et vita eorum a criminibus intacta sit et res ab iniuria improborum. Qui autem his praeceptis resultaverint, monasteriis deputentur, ut vagantes animi et superbi severiori regula distringantur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p30","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXIIII. Ignorance the mother of all errors, especially in the priests of God is to be avoided, who the office of teaching among the peoples have received. Priests indeed to read the holy Scriptures frequently the apostle Paul admonishes, saying to Timothy: Attend to reading, to exhortation. Let teachers always remain in these things themselves know. Therefore priests the holy Scriptures and canons let them meditate, that every work of them in preaching and doctrine may consist, and they may edify all both by the knowledge of faith and by the discipline of works.","Ignorantia mater cunctorum errorum, maxime in sacerdotibus dei vitanda est, qui docendi officium in populis susceperunt. Sacerdotes enim legere sanctas scripturas frequenter admonet Paulus apostolus dicens ad Timotheum: Intende lectioni, exhortationi. Doctores semper manere in his se sciant. Igitur sacerdotes scripturas sanctas et canones meditentur, ut omne opus eorum in praedicatione et doctrina consistat atque aedificent cunctos tam fidei scientia quam operum disciplina."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p31","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXV. When presbyters in parishes are ordained, let them receive a little official book from their priest, that to the churches deputed to them instructed they may succeed, lest through ignorance even in the divine sacraments themselves they may offend, so that, when to the litanies or to the council they shall have come, they may render an account to their bishop, how they celebrate the received office or baptize.","Quando presbyteri in parrochiis ordinantur, libellum officialem a sacerdote suo accipiant, ut ad ecclesias sibi deputatas instructi succedant, ne per ignorantiam etiam in ipsis divinis sacramentis offendant, ut, quando ad laetanias vel ad concilium venerint, rationem episcopo suo reddant, qualiter susceptum officium celebrent vel baptizent."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p32","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXVI. When presbyters or deacons through parishes are constituted, it behooves them to make profession to their bishop, that chastely and purely they may live under the fear of God, that while by such profession he shall have bound them, holy discipline he may retain.","Quando presbyteri aut diaconi per parrochias constituuntur, oportet eos professionem episcopo suo facere, ut caste et pure vivant sub dei timore, ut dum eos tali professione obligaverit, sanctam disciplinam retineat."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p33","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXVII. A bishop, presbyter, or deacon, if from his grade unjustly cast down in a second synod innocent be found, cannot be that which he was, unless the grades lost he may receive before the altar from the hand of bishops: if a bishop he is, the orarium, ring, and staff; if a presbyter, the orarium and planet; if a deacon, the orarium and alb; if a subdeacon, the paten and chalice; thus also the remaining grades in the reparation of themselves let them receive those things which, when they were ordained, they had received.","Episcopus, presbyter aut diaconus, si a gradu suo iniuste deiectus in secunda synodo innocens reperiatur, non potest esse, quod fuerat, nisi gradus amissos recipiat coram altario de manu episcoporum, si episcopus est, orarium, anulum et baculum, si presbyter, orarium et planetam, si diaconus, orarium et albam, si subdiaconus, patenam et calicem, sic et reliqui gradus ea in reparationem sui recipiant, quae, cum ordinarentur, perceperant."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p34","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXVIII. If any bishop or presbyter or deacon or any from the order of clerics magicians or haruspices or soothsayers or certainly augurs or sorcerers or those who profess any magical art or anyone of them similar things practicing to have consulted shall have been detected, from the honor of his dignity deposed, let him receive the charge of a monastery and there, perpetually penance given, the crime committed of sacrilege let him atone.","Si episcopus quis aut presbyter sive diaconus vel quilibet ex ordine clericorum magos aut aruspices aut ariolos aut certe augures vel sortilegos vel eos, qui profitentur artem aliquam aut aliquis eorum similia exercentes consuluisse fuerit depraehensus, ab honore dignitatis suae depositus monasterii curam excipiat ibique perpetua poenitentia deditus scelus admissum sacrilegii solvat."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p35","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXVIIII. Bishops neighboring to enemies besides those who from the royal power have received license, by anyone a mandate from a foreign nation secretly to receive or to send let them not presume. Who moreover is detected or is convicted, denounced to the prince, before the council with condign punishment let him be punished.","Confinitimi hostium sacerdotes praeter eos, qui a regia potestate licentiam acceperunt, a quolibet mandatum a gente extraneum occulte accipere vel dirigere non praesumant. Qui autem depraehenditur aut convincitur denuntiatus principi apud concilium condigna animadversione multabitur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p36","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXX. Often princes against any liable to the crime of majesty their affairs to priests commit. And because priests by Christ to the ministry of salvation are chosen, there let them consent to be made judges for kings, where by oath the indulgence of punishment is promised, not where the sentence of peril is prepared. If anyone therefore of the priests against this common counsel a discerner in the perils of others shall have been, let him be guilty of shed blood before Christ, and before the church let him lose his own grade.","Saepe principes contra quoslibet maiestatis obnoxios sacerdotibus negotia sua committunt. Et quia sacerdotes a Christo ad ministerium salutis electi sunt, ibi consentiant regibus fieri iudices, ubi iureiurando supplicii indulgentia promittitur, non ubi discriminis sententia praeparatur. Si quis ergo sacerdotum contra hoc commune consultum discussor in alienis periculis exstiterit, sit reus effusi sanguinis apud Christum et apud ecclesiam perdat proprium gradum."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p37","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXXI. Bishops in protecting the peoples and defending the imposed from God to themselves care let them not doubt. Therefore when they see judges and powerful men to be oppressors of the poor, first them by priestly admonition let them reprove, and if they shall have despised to be amended, their insolence to the royal ears let them make known, that those whom priestly admonition does not bend to justice, the royal power from iniquity may coerce. If anyone moreover of the bishops this shall have neglected, before the council let him be guilty.","Episcopi in protegendis populis ac defendendis impositam a deo sibi curam non ambigant, ideoque dum conspiciunt iudices ac potentes pauperum oppressores exsistere, prius eos sacerdotali admonitione redarguant, et si contempserint emendari, eorum insolentiam regiis auribus intiment, ut, quos sacerdotalis admonitio inflectit ad iustitiam, regalis potestas ab improbitate coerceat. Si quis autem episcoporum id neglexerit, concilio reus erit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p38","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXXII. Avarice is the root of all evils, whose thirst even the minds of priests occupies. For many of the faithful in the love of Christ and of the martyrs in the parishes of bishops basilicas build, holy offerings give; priests these take away and into their own uses convert. From this it is that the cultivators of sacred things fail, while their stipends they lose; from this the ruins of falling basilicas are not repaired, because by priestly avarice all things are taken away. For which thing it has been established by this council that bishops so rule their dioceses, that nothing from their right they presume to take away, but according to the authority of the prior councils, both of offerings and of tributes and of crops, let them obtain the third part. But if anything more by them shall have been presumed, through the council let it be restored, the founders themselves or certainly their relatives appealing, if now they from the world have departed. Moreover let the founders of basilicas know that in the things which to the same churches they confer, they have no power, but according to the institutes of the canons, as the church, so also its dowry, to the ordinance of the bishop pertains.","Avaritia radix est cunctorum malorum, cuius sitis etiam sacerdotum mentes obtinet. Multi enim fidelium in amore Christi et martyrum in parrochiis episcoporum basilicas construunt, oblationes sanctas tribuunt, sacerdotes haec auferunt atque in usus suos convertunt. Inde est, quod cultores sacrorum deficiunt, dum stipendia sua perdunt, inde labentium basilicarum ruinae non reparantur, quia avaritia sacerdotali omnia auferuntur. Pro qua re constitutum est a praesenti concilio episcopos ita dioceses suas regere, ut nihil ex earum iure praesumant auferre, sed iuxta priorum auctoritatem conciliorum tam de oblationibus quam de tributis ac frugibus tertiam consequantur. Quod si amplius quippiam ab eis praesumptum exstiterit, per concilium restauretur appellantibus aut ipsis conditoribus aut certe propinquis eorum, si iam illi a saeculo discesserunt. Noverint autem conditores basilicarum in rebus, quas eisdem ecclesiis conferunt, nullam potestatem habere, sed iuxta canonum instituta sicut ecclesiam ita et dos eius ad ordinationem episcopi pertineat."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p39","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXXIII. Whoever bishop another bishop's diocese for thirty years without any interpellation shall have possessed, because according to the right of the law his it is seen to be the diocese, not to be admitted against him an action of reclaiming, but this within one parish, outside indeed in no way, lest while a diocese is defended, the boundaries of provinces may be confused.","Quicumque episcopus alterius episcopi diocesim per triginta annos sine aliqua interpellatione possederit, quia secundum ius legis eius videtur esse diocesis, admittenda non est contra eum actio reposcendi, sed hoc intra unam parrochiam, extra vero nullo modo, ne dum diocesis defenditur, provinciarum termini confundantur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p40","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXXIIII. Just as a diocese another's by a thirty-year possession is taken away, so the jurisdiction of territory does it not admit. Therefore basilicas which newly have been built, to him without doubt the bishop will pertain, whose the jurisdiction it shall have been established to be.","Sicut diocesim alienam trigennalis possessio tollit, ita territorii conventum non admittit. Ideoque basilicae, quae novae conditae fuerint, ad eum procul dubio episcopum pertinebunt, cuius conventus esse constiterit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p41","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXXV. A bishop through all the dioceses and his parishes by each single year to go it behooves, that he may search out what each basilica in its repair needs. But if he himself either by languor detained or by other occupations implicated this to fulfill shall not have been able, let him send presbyters of probity or deacons, who both the returns of the basilicas and the repairs and the life of the ministers may search out.","Episcopum per cunctas dioceses parrochiasque suas per singulos annos ire oportet, ut exquirat, quid unaquaeque basilica in reparationem sui indiget. Quod si ipse aut languore detentus aut aliis occupationibus implicatus id explere nequiverit, presbyteros probabiles aut diacones mittat, qui et reditus basilicarum et reparationes et ministrantium vitam inquirant."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p42","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXXVI. Whoever bishops by the support of anyone anything of ecclesiastical utility shall have provided and for that whatever convenience in remuneration they shall have promised, let it behoove them to pay the solution of the promise, so that that to the provincial council be referred, that by their concurrence it may be confirmed, because, as Paul says: Worthy is the laborer of his reward.","Quicumque episcopi suffragio cuiuslibet aliquid ecclesiasticae utilitatis providerint et pro eo quodcumque commodum in remunerationem promiserint, promissi solutionem eos exsolvere oportebit, ita ut id ad concilium comprovinciale deferatur, ut eorum conhibentia confirmetur, quia, sicut Paulus ait: Dignus est operarius mercede sua."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p43","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXXVII. To be supplied is by the priests solace of life to the needy and especially to those to whom a return is to be made. Whoever therefore of the faithful from their own goods to the church anything by their own devotion shall have conferred, if perhaps they themselves or their children shall have been reduced to poverty, from the same church let them receive support of life for the use of the time. For if to clerics or monks or pilgrims or to anyone suffering necessity on account of religion alone for use the ecclesiastical goods are given, how much more is to be provided for those to whom a just retribution is owed?","Praebendum est a sacerdotibus vitae solacium indigentibus et maxime his, quibus restituenda vicissitudo est. Quicumque ergo fidelium de facultatibus suis ecclesiae aliquid devotione propria contulerint, si forte ipsi aut filii eorum redacti fuerint ad inopiam, ab eadem ecclesia suffragium vitae pro temporis usu percipiant. Si enim clericis vel monachis seu peregrinis aut quamlibet necessitatem sustinentibus pro solo religionis intuitu in usum res ecclesiasticae largiuntur, quanto magis consulendum est, quibus retributio iusta debetur?"],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p44","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXXVIII. Not a few deacons into so great pride burst forth, that themselves before presbyters they place and in the first choir themselves first to stand presume, with presbyters in the second choir constituted. Therefore that they may know the presbyters to be more elevated than themselves, let both these and those in each choir stand.","Nonnulli diacones in tantam erumpunt superbiam, ut sese presbyteris anteponant atque in primo choro ipsi priores stare praesumant, presbyteris in secundo choro constitutis. Ergo ut sublimiores se presbyteros agnoscant, tam hi quam illi in utroque choro consistant."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p45","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XXXIX. If two oraria not even to a bishop is it permitted to put on nor to a presbyter, how much less to a deacon, who is their minister. One therefore orarium it behooves a levite to bear on the left shoulder, on account of which he prays, that is he preaches; the right part moreover it behooves him to have free, that unencumbered to the priestly ministry he may run. Let them beware therefore henceforth levites to use a double orarium, but only one and pure, nor with any colors or gold ornamented.","Si oraria duo nec episcopo quidem induere licet nec presbytero, utique multo minus diacono, qui minister eorum est. Unum igitur orarium oportet levitam gestare in sinistro humero, propter quod orat, id est praedicat; dexteram autem partem oportet habere liberam, ut expeditus ad ministerium sacerdotale discurrat. Caveant igitur amodo levitae geminum uti orarium, sed unum tantum et purum nec ullis coloribus aut auro ornatum."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p46","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XL. All clerics or readers, just as levites and priests, with the whole head shorn above, below only the circle's crown let them leave, not as hitherto in the parts of Gaul readers are seen to do, who with hair long as laymen on the sole top of the head a small circle they shave; for this rite in Spain hitherto of heretics has been. Whence it is fitting that for removing from the churches scandal this sign of disgrace be taken away and one be the tonsure or habit as the use of all Spain is. Who moreover this shall not have guarded, of the catholic faith let him be guilty.","Omnes clerici vel lectores sicut levitae et sacerdotes detonso superius toto capite, inferius solam circuli coronam relinquant, non sicut hucusque in Galliciae partibus facere lectores videntur, qui prolixis ut laici comis in solo capitis apice modicum circulum tondunt; ritus enim iste in Spaniis hucusque hereticorum fuit. Unde oportet, ut pro amputando ab ecclesiis scandalo hoc signum dedecoris auferatur et una sit tonsura vel habitus sicut totius Spaniae est usus. Qui autem hoc non custodierit, fidei catholicae reus erit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p47","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XLI. With clerics let extraneous women by no means dwell, except only mother and sister, daughter, aunt, in which persons nothing of crime the bond of nature permits to be esteemed. For this also the constitution of the ancient fathers decreed.","Cum clericis extraneae feminae nullatenus habitent, nisi tantum mater et soror, filia, amita, in quibus personis nihil sceleris aestimari foedus naturae permittit. Id enim et constitutio antiquorum patrum decrevit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p48","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XLII. Some clerics, not having legitimate marriage, of extraneous women or of their own maidservants the forbidden for themselves consortia seek. Therefore those who with clerics thus have been joined, let them be taken away by the bishop and sold, with those for the time being relegated to penance whom their lust has infected.","Quidam clerici legitime non habentes coniugium extranearum mulierum vel ancillarum suarum interdicta sibi consortia appetunt, ideo, quae cum clericis taliter coniunctae sunt, ab episcopo auferantur et venundentur, illis pro tempore religatis ad poenitentiam, quos sua libidine infecerunt."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p49","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XLIII. Clerics who without the consultation of their bishop wives have taken or a widow or a divorced woman or a prostitute into marriage have received, let it behoove them to be separated by their own bishop.","Clerici, qui sine consultu episcopi sui uxores duxerint aut viduam vel repudiatam vel meretricem in coniugium acceperint, separari eos a proprio episcopo oportebit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p50","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XLIV. Clerics in any sedition arms willingly shall have taken or have taken, found, having lost the grade of their order into a monastery for penance let them be given.","Clerici in quacumque seditione arma volentes sumpserint aut sumpserunt, reperti amisso ordinis sui gradu in monasterium poenitentiae contradantur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p51","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XLV. If any cleric in demolishing sepulchers shall have been detected, because this crime for sacrilege by the public laws with blood is avenged, it behooves that by the canons in such a crime detected from the clerical order he be removed and to penance for three years be assigned.","Si quis clericus in demoliendis sepulchris fuerit depraehensus, quia facinus hoc pro sacrilegio legibus publicis sanguine vindicatur, oportet canonibus in tali scelere proditum a clericatus ordine submoveri et poenitentiae triennio deputari."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p52","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XLVI. Our Lord and most excellent King Sisenand commanding, this the holy council established, that all ingenuous clerics on account of the office of religion from every public indiction and labor be immune, that free they may serve God and by no fore-mentioned necessity from ecclesiastical offices may they be drawn back.","Praecipiente domino nostro atque excellentissimo Sisenando rege id constituit sanctum concilium, ut omnes ingenui clerici pro officio religionis ab omni publica indictione atque labore habeant immunes, ut liberi deo serviant nullaque praedicti necessitate ab ecclesiasticis officiis retrahantur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p53","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XLVII. Those whom the Greeks call \"oeconomos,\" that is those who in the place of bishops the ecclesiastical goods administer, just as the holy synod of Chalcedon established, all bishops from their own clergy for ruling the churches ought to have. Who moreover from now on shall have despised, liable of the same great council he will be.","Eos, quos oeconomos Graeci vocant, hoc est, qui vice episcoporum res ecclesiasticas tractant, sicut sancta synodus Chalcedonensis instituit, omnes episcopos de proprio clero ad regendas ecclesias habere oportet. Qui autem deinceps contempserit, obnoxius eiusdem magni concilii erit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p54","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XLVIII. A monk either paternal devotion or his own profession makes. Whichever of these shall have been, that which has been chosen he will hold. Therefore to these the way to return to the world we close and all to the world we forbid regresses.","Monachum aut paterna devotio aut propria professio facit. Quicquid horum fuerit, allegatum tenebit. Proinde his ad mundum reverti intercludimus aditum et omnes ad saeculum interdicimus regressus."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p55","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","XLIX. Clerics who seek to become provosts of monks, because a better life they desire to follow, let free to them by the bishop into monasteries be granted to enter, nor let the purpose of those be forbidden who to the desire of contemplation to pass over strive.","Clerici, qui monachorum praepositum appetunt, quia meliorem vitam sequi cupiunt, liberos eis ab episcopo in monasteriis largiri oportet ingressus, nec interdici propositum eorum, qui ad contemplationis desiderium transire nituntur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p56","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","L. It has been announced to this council that monks by episcopal command to servile work are subjected and the rights of monasteries against the constitutions of the canons by illicit presumption are usurped, so that almost from a coenobium a possession is made and the illustrious portion of Christ to ignominy and servitude comes. On which account we warn those who preside over churches, that henceforth such things they not presume, but this only for themselves in a monastery let the priests claim, that which the canons command, that is, to admonish monks to holy conversation, to appoint abbots and other offices, and things done beyond the rule to correct. But if anything in monks by the canons forbidden they shall have presumed or to usurp anything from the goods of the monastery they shall have tried, there will not be wanting against them the sentence of excommunication, who themselves from now on by no means shall have removed themselves from illicit things.","Nuntiatum est praesenti concilio, quod monachi episcopali imperio servili opere mancipentur et iura monasteriorum contra constituta canonum illicita praesumptione usurpentur, ita ut pene ex coenobio possessio fiat atque illustris portio Christi ad ignominiam servitutemque perveniat. Quapropter monemus eos, qui ecclesiis praesunt, ut ultra talia non praesumant, sed hoc tantum sibi in monasterio vindicent sacerdotes, quod praecipiunt canones, id est, monachos ad conversationem sanctam praemonere, abbates aliaque officia instituere atque extra regulam facta corrigere. Quod si aliquid in monachis canonibus interdictum praesumpserint aut usurpare quippiam de monasterii rebus temptaverint, non deerit ab illis sententia excommunicationis, qui se deinceps nequaquam sustulerint ab illicitis."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p57","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LI. Not a few monks, going forth from a monastery, not only to the world return, but even also wives take. Therefore let them be recalled into the same monastery from which they went forth, to penance let them be assigned, and there let them bewail their crimes, from which they departed.","Nonnulli monachorum egredientes a monasterio non solum ad saeculum revertuntur, sed etiam et uxores accipiunt. Igitur revocati in eodem monasterio, a quo exierant, poenitentiae deputentur ibique defleant crimina sua, unde discesserunt."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p58","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LII. The religious of their own region, who neither among clerics nor among monks are held, whether those who through diverse places wandering are borne, by the bishops in whose jurisdiction they are known to abide, by their license let them be coerced, that either in the clergy or in a monastery they be assigned, besides those who by their bishop either on account of languor have been absolved.","Religiosi propriae regionis, qui nec inter clericos nec inter monachos habentur, sive hi, qui per diversa loca vagi feruntur, ab episcopis, in quorum conventu commorari noscuntur, licentia eorum coerceatur, ut aut in clero aut in monasterio deputentur praeter eos, qui ab episcopo suo aut propter languorem fuerint absoluti."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p59","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LIII. Those who in peril constituted receive penance, no manifest crimes confessing, but only themselves sinners being proclaiming, of this sort, if they shall have recovered, can even by probity of morals to ecclesiastical grades come. Who indeed so receive penance that any mortal sin to have perpetrated they publicly confess, to the clergy or ecclesiastical honors to come by no means will be able, because themselves by their own confession they have noted.","Hi, qui in discrimine constituti poenitentiam accipiunt, nulla manifesta scelera confitentes, sed tantum se peccatores esse praedicantes, huiusmodi, si revaluerint, possunt etiam per morum probitatem ad gradus ecclesiasticos pervenire. Qui vero ita poenitentiam accipiunt, ut aliquod mortale peccatum perpetrasse publice fateantur, ad clerum vel honores ecclesiasticos pervenire nullatenus poterunt, quia se confessione propria notaverunt."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p60","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LIIII. Whoever from laymen receiving penance have tonsured themselves and again prevaricating have been made laymen, detected by their bishop to the penance from which they withdrew let them be recalled. But if any by power are irrevocable nor being admonished return, truly as apostates before the church by the sentence of anathema let them be condemned. Not otherwise also those who have been tonsured by parents or by their own will, the parents having been lost, themselves to religion have devoted and afterwards the secular habit have assumed, and the same by a priest detected to the cultivation of religion, penance first having been done, let them be recalled. But if they are not able to return, as by the power of anathema let them be subjected to sentence. Which form will be guarded also in widows and sacred virgins and penitent women, who the holy habit have put on and afterwards either have changed the garment or to marriages have passed over.","Quicumque ex saecularibus accipientes poenitentiam totonderunt se et rursus praevaricantes laici effecti sunt, compraehensi ab episcopo suo ad poenitentiam, ex qua recesserunt, revocentur. Quod si aliqui per potentiam irrevocabiles sunt nec admoniti revertuntur, vere ut apostatae coram ecclesiam anathematis sententia condemnentur. Non aliter et hi, qui detonsi a parentibus fuerint aut sponte sua amissis parentibus seipsos religioni devoverunt et postea habitum saecularem sumpserunt et idem a sacerdote compraehensi ad cultum religionis acta prius poenitentia revocentur. Quod si reverti non possunt, velut a potestate anathematis sententiae subiciantur. Quae forma servabitur etiam in viduis virginibusque sacris ac poenitentibus feminis, quae sanctimonialem habitum induerunt et postea aut vestem mutaverunt aut ad nuptias transierunt."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p61","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LV. There are two kinds of widows: secular and holy. Secular widows are those who, still intending to marry, the lay habit have not put off. Holy widows are those who, having already changed the secular habit, under religious cultivation in the sight of the priest or of the church have appeared. These, if to marriages they shall have passed over, according to the apostle not without damnation they will be, because themselves first to God having vowed afterwards the purpose of chastity have cast off.","Duo sunt genera viduarum, saeculares et sanctimoniales. Saeculares viduae sunt, quae adhuc disponentes nubere laicalem habitum non deposuerunt; sanctimoniales sunt, quae iam mutato habitu saeculari sub religioso cultu in conspectu sacerdotis vel ecclesiae apparuerunt. Hae, si ad nuptias transierint, iuxta apostolum non sine damnatione erunt, quia se primum deo voventes postea castitatis propositum abiecerunt."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p62","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LVI. Concerning the Jews moreover this holy synod commanded that to no one from now on to believe violence be inflicted. For on whom God wills, he has mercy, and whom he wills, he hardens. For not such unwilling are to be saved, but willing, that the form of justice may be whole. For just as man by the will of his own choice obeying the serpent perished, so by the grace of God calling by the conversion of his own mind each man believing is saved. Therefore not by violence, but by the free faculty of the will, that they may be converted, they are to be persuaded, not rather to be impelled. Who moreover already long ago to Christianity have been compelled, as was done in the times of the most religious prince Sisebut, because it now stands that they have been associated with the divine sacraments and have received the grace of baptism and have been anointed with chrism and of the body of the Lord and of the blood have been partakers, it is fitting that the faith, which even by force or necessity they received, to keep they be compelled, lest the name of the Lord be blasphemed and the faith which they received be esteemed vile and contemptible.","De Iudaeis autem praecepit sancta synodus nemini deinceps ad credendum vim inferre. Cui enim vult deus miseretur, et quem vult, indurat. Non enim tales inviti salvandi sunt, sed volentes, ut integra sit forma iustitiae. Sicut enim homo propria arbitrii voluntate serpenti oboediens periit, sic vocante se gratia dei propriae mentis conversione homo quisque credendo salvatur. Ergo non vi, sed libera arbitrii facultate, ut convertantur, suadendi sunt, non potius impellendi. Qui autem iam pridem ad christianitatem coacti sunt, sicut factum est temporibus religiosissimi principis Sisebuti, quia iam constat eos sacramentis divinis associatos et baptismi gratiam suscepisse et chrismate unctos esse et corporis domini et sanguinis exstitisse participes, oportet, ut fidem, etiam quam vi vel necessitate susceperunt, tenere cogantur, ne nomen domini blasphemetur et fides, quam susceperunt, vilis ac contemptibilis habeatur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p63","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LVII. So great is the cupidity of some, that some seeking it, according to that which the apostle says, even from the faith have erred. For many hitherto from the priests and from laymen, receiving from Jews gifts, their perfidy by their patronage foster, who not undeservedly from the body of antichrist are known to be, because against Christ they act. Whoever therefore from now on a bishop or cleric or secular to them against the Christian faith support either by gift or by favor shall have provided, truly as profane and sacrilegious, anathema made, from the catholic church and from the kingdom of God let him be made a stranger, because worthy he is that from the body of Christ he be separated, who to the enemies of Christ a patron is made.","Tanta est quorumdam cupiditas, ut quidam eam appetentes, iuxta quod ait apostolus, etiam a fide erraverint. Multi quippe hucusque ex sacerdotibus atque laicis accipientes a Iudaeis munera, perfidiam eorum patrocinio suo foveant, qui non immerito ex corpore antichristi esse noscuntur, quia contra Christum faciunt. Quicumque igitur deinceps episcopus sive clericus sive saecularis illis contra fidem christianam suffragium vel munere vel favore praestiterit, vere ut profanus et sacrilegus anathema effectus ab ecclesia catholica et regno dei efficiatur extraneus, quia dignus est, ut a corpore Christi separetur, qui inimicis Christi patronus efficitur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p64","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LVIII. Very many who from the Jews long ago to the Christian faith have been promoted, now blaspheming Christ not only the Jewish rites to have perpetrated are known, but even the abominable circumcisions to practice have presumed. Concerning whom by the counsel of the most pious and most religious lord our King Sisenand, this holy council decreed, that transgressors of this sort by pontifical authority corrected to the cultivation of Christian dogma be recalled, that those whom their own will does not amend, the animadversion of the priest may coerce. Those moreover whom they have circumcised, if their sons they are, from the consortium of the parents let them be separated; if slaves, on account of the injury of their body to liberty let them be given.","Plerique, qui ex Iudaeis dudum ad christianam fidem promoti sunt, nunc blasphemantes Christo non solum iudaicos ritus perpetrasse noscuntur, sed etiam abominandas circumcisiones exercere praesumpserunt, de quibus consultu piissimi ac religiosissimi domini nostri Sisenandi regis hoc sanctum decrevit concilium, ut huiusmodi transgressores pontificali auctoritate correcti ad cultum christiani dogmatis revocentur, ut, quos voluntas propria non emendat, animadversio sacerdotalis coerceat. Eos autem, quos circumciderunt, si filii eorum sunt, a parentum consortio separentur, si servi, pro iniuria corporis sui libertati tradantur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p65","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LVIIII. The sons and daughters of Jews, lest of parents further they may be involved in errors, from their consortium to be separated we decree, assigned either to monasteries or to Christian men and women fearing God, that under their conversation the cultivation of faith they may learn and better instructed both in morals and in faith may they profit.","Iudaeorum filios vel filias, ne parentum ultra involvantur erroribus, ab eorum consortio separari decernimus deputatos aut monasteriis aut christianis viris ac mulieribus deum timentibus, ut sub eorum conversatione cultum fidei discant atque in melius instituti tam in moribus quam in fide proficiant."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p66","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LX. Jews having been baptized, if afterwards prevaricating against Christ by any penalty have been damned, from their goods the faithful sons to be excluded it will not behoove, because it is written: The son shall not bear the iniquity of the father.","Iudaei baptizati, si postea praevaricantes in Christum qualibet poena damnati exstiterint, a rebus eorum fideles filios excludi non oportebit, quia scriptum est: Filius non portabit iniquitatem patris."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p67","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXI. Often the consortia of evil men even the good corrupt, how much more those who to vices are prone? Let no further communion therefore be of Hebrews to the Christian faith transferred with those who still in the old rite remain, lest by their participation they be subverted. Whoever therefore from now on of those who have been baptized, the consortia of the unfaithful shall not have avoided, let both these be given to Christians, and those be assigned to public beatings.","Saepe malorum consortia etiam bonos corrumpunt, quanto magis eos, qui ad vitia proni sunt? Nulla igitur ultra communio sit Hebraeis ad fidem christianam translatis cum his, qui adhuc in veteri ritu consistunt, ne forte eorum participio subvertantur. Quicumque igitur amodo ex his, qui baptizati sunt, infidelium consortia non vitaverint, et hi christianis donentur, et illi publicis caedibus deputentur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p68","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXII. Jews who have Christian women in marriage, let them be admonished by the bishop of that city, that if with them to remain they desire, let them become Christians. But if admonished they shall have been unwilling, let them be separated, because the unfaithful cannot remain in the union of her who already into the Christian faith has been transferred. Sons moreover who from such have been born, the faith and condition of the mother let them follow. Similarly also those who have been procreated from unfaithful women and faithful men, let them follow the Christian religion, not the Jewish superstition.","Iudaei, qui christianas mulieres in coniugio habent, admoneantur ab episcopo civitatis ipsius, ut, si cum eis permanere cupiunt, christiani efficiantur. Quod si admoniti noluerint, separentur, quia non potest infidelis in eius permanere coniunctione, quae iam in christianam translata est fidem. Filii autem, qui ex talibus nati exsistunt, fidem atque condicionem matris sequantur. Similiter et hi, qui procreati sunt de infidelibus mulieribus et fidelibus viris, christianam sequantur religionem, non iudaicam superstitionem."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p69","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXIII. Not can he be toward men faithful, who toward God shall have been unfaithful. Jews therefore who long ago have been made Christians and now against the faith of Christ have prevaricated, to giving testimony to be admitted ought not, although themselves to be Christians they announce, because as in the faith of Christ they are suspect, so also in human testimony let them be held doubtful. To be weakened therefore it behooves the testimony of those who in the faith are proven false, nor to them is to be believed who the truth of faith from themselves have cast off.","Non potest erga homines esse fidelis, qui deo exstiterit infidus. Iudaei ergo, qui dudum christiani effecti sunt et nunc in Christi fidem praevaricati sunt, ad testimonium dicendum admitti non debent, quamvis esse se christianos annuntient, quia sicut in Christi fide suspecti sunt, ita in testimonio humano dubii habentur. Infirmari ergo oportet eorum testimonium, qui in fide falsi docentur, nec eis esse credendum, qui veritatis a se fidem abiecerunt."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p70","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXIIII. Our Lord and most excellent King Sisenand commanding, this holy council established, that Jews or those who from Jews are, public offices by no means seek, because under this occasion to Christians injury they do. Therefore let the judges of the provinces with the priests their fraudulent suppressions put aside suspend, and let them not permit them to perform public offices. If anyone moreover this shall have permitted, as against a sacrilegious let excommunication be brought forth, and he who shall have suppressed it, to public beatings let him be assigned.","Praecipiente domino atque excellentissimo Sisenando rege id constituit sanctum concilium, ut Iudaei aut hi, qui ex Iudaeis sunt, officia publica nullatenus appetant, quia sub hac occasione christianis iniuriam faciunt. Ideoque iudices provinciarum cum sacerdotibus eorum subreptiones fraudulenter relictas suspendant et officia publica eos agere non permittant. Si quis autem hoc permiserit, velut in sacrilegum excommunicatio proferatur, et is, qui suppresserit, publicis caedibus deputetur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p71","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXV. By the decree of the most glorious prince this holy council chose, that it not be lawful for Jews to have Christian slaves nor Christian servants to purchase nor by anyone's largess to obtain. For it is nefarious that the members of Christ serve the ministers of antichrist. But if from now on Christian slaves or maidservants the Jews shall have presumed to have, let them be taken away from their dominion and let them obtain liberty from the prince.","Ex decreto gloriosissimi principis hoc sanctum elegit concilium, ut Iudaeis non liceat christianos servos nec christiana mancipia emere nec cuiusquam consequi largitate. Nefas est enim, ut membra Christi serviant antichristi ministris. Quod si deinceps servos christianos vel ancillas Iudaei habere praesumpserint, sublati ab eorum dominatu libertatem a principe consequantur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p72","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXVI. And if those who nothing from their own goods to the poor of Christ distribute, by the voice of the eternal Judge in the future will be condemned, how much more those who take away from the poor what they have not given. On which account bishops who nothing from their own to the church of Christ have compensated, this divine sentence let them fear, and from the servants of the church for their own condemnation freedmen to make let them not presume. Impious it is indeed, that he who his own goods to the church of Christ has not conferred, loss should inflict and the right of the church to alienate should contend. Such therefore freedmen let the successor bishop without any opposition to the right of the church recall, because them not equity but improbity absolved.","Et si illi, qui nulla ex rebus suis pauperibus Christi distribuunt, aeterni iudicis voce in futuro condemnabuntur, quanto magis hi, qui auferunt pauperibus, quod non dederunt. Quapropter episcopi, qui nihil ex proprio suo ecclesiae Christi compensaverunt, hanc divinam sententiam metuant et liberos ex famulis ecclesiae ad condemnationem suam facere non praesumant. Impium est enim, ut, qui res suas ecclesiae Christi non contulerit, damnum inferat et ius ecclesiae alienare contendat. Tales igitur libertos successor episcopus absque aliqua oppositione ad ius ecclesiae revocabit, quia eos non aequitas sed improbitas absolvit."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p73","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXVII. A bishop who desires to manumit a servant of the right of the church, the ecclesiastical patronage not having been retained, let him offer two of his own property both of the same merit and of the peculium before the council of the church over which he presides, by the subscribing priests in exchange, that the definition of the exchanger may be found ratified and just. For then he will be able to grant free manumission without the patronage of the church, because him whom to liberty to hand over he disposes, now to his own right he has acquired. Moreover to a freedman of this sort against the church, of whose right he has been, of accusing or testifying let license be denied. But if he shall have presumed, it pleases that, the exchange standing, into the servitude of his own church he be recalled, which he tries to harm.","Episcopus, qui mancipium iuris ecclesiae non retento ecclesiastico patrocinio manumitti desiderat, duo meriti eiusdem et peculii coram concilio ecclesiae, cui praeminet, per commutationem subscribentibus sacerdotibus offerat, ut rata et iusta inveniatur definitio commutantis. Tunc enim liberam manumissionem sine patrocinio ecclesiae concedere poterit, quia eum, quem libertati tradere disponit, iam iuri proprio acquisivit. Huiusmodi autem liberto adversus ecclesiam, cuius iuris exstitit, accusandi vel testificandi denegetur licentia. Quod si praesumpserit, placet, ut stante commutatione in servitutem propriae ecclesiae revocetur, quam nocere conatur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p74","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXVIII. The consent of the whole council defined, that priests who either their own goods to the church leave or, having nothing, yet some estates or families for their churches acquire, it will be lawful for them some from the families of the same church to manumit according to the measure of the thing conferred, which the ancient canons decreed, so that with their peculium and their posterity let them remain free under the patronage of the church, the enjoined utilities according to that which they shall have been able pursuing.","Consensus totius concilii definivit, ut sacerdotes, qui aut res suas ecclesiae relinquunt aut nihil habentes aliqua tamen praedia aut familias ecclesiis suis conquirunt, licebit illis aliquos de familiis eiusdem ecclesiae manumittere iuxta rei collatae modum, quem antiqui canones decreverunt, ita ut cum peculio et posteritate sua ingenui sub patrocinio ecclesiae maneant, utilitates iniunctas sibi, iuxta quod potuerint, prosequentes."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p75","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXVIIII. Freedmen of the church, because never dies their patroness, from the patronage of the same never let them depart, nor their posterity indeed, as the prior canons decreed. And lest perhaps their liberty in future offspring might not be clear and the posterity itself by natural ingenuousness striving might from the patronage of the church not withdraw themselves, it is necessary that both the same freedmen and those procreated from them make profession to their bishop, by which from the family of the church free made themselves to be they confess, and its patronage not leave, but according to their ability service or obedience to it they provide.","Liberti ecclesiae, quia numquam moritur eorum patrona, a patrocinio eiusdem numquam discedant nec posteritas quidem eorum, sicut priores canones decreverunt. Ac ne forte libertas eorum in futura prole non pateat ipsaque posteritas naturali ingenuitate obnitens sese ab ecclesiae patrocinio non subtrahat, necesse est, ut tam idem liberti, quam ab eis progeniti professionem episcopo suo faciant, per quam ex familia ecclesiae liberos effectos se esse fateantur eiusque patrocinium non relinquant, sed iuxta virtutem suam obsequium ei vel oboedientiam praebeant."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p76","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXX. Freedmen of the church who from its patronage departing to any persons have adhered, if admonished to return they shall have despised, let their manumission be void, because by the contempt of inobedience they are held by the action of ingratitude.","Liberti ecclesiae, qui a patrocinio eius discedentes quibuslibet personis adhaeserunt, si admoniti redire contempserint, manumissio eorum irrita sit, quia per inoboedientiae contemptum ingrati actione tenentur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p77","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXXI. Freedmen who by any persons have been manumitted and to the patronage of the church commended exist, as the rules of the ancient fathers established, by priestly defense from the insolence of anyone let them be protected, whether in the state of their liberty or in the peculium which they are known to have.","Liberti, qui a quibuscumque manumissi sunt atque ecclesiae patrocinio commendati exsistunt, sicut regulae antiquorum patrum constituerunt, sacerdotali defensione a cuiuslibet insolentia protegantur sive in statu libertatis eorum seu in peculio, quod habere noscuntur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p78","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXXII. Whoever receive liberty from their masters in such a way that no service in them for themselves the patron retains, these, if without crime they are, to the clerical order let them be freely received, because by direct manumission they are known to be absolved. Who indeed with retained service have been manumitted, because still to the service of the patron they are held liable, by no means are they to be promoted to the ecclesiastical order, lest, when they shall have willed, their masters may be made from clerics servants.","Quicumque libertatem a dominis suis ita percipiunt, ut nullum sibi in eis obsequium patronus retentet, isti, si sine crimine sunt, ad clericatus ordinem liberi suscipiantur, quia directa manumissione absoluti noscuntur. Qui vero retento obsequio manumissi sunt, pro eo, quod adhuc patroni servitute tenentur obnoxii, nullatenus sunt ad ecclesiasticum ordinem promovendi, ne, quando voluerint, eorum domini fiant ex clericis servi."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p79","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXXIII. From the families of the church to constitute presbyters and deacons through the parishes it is permitted, whom nevertheless the rectitude of life and probity of morals commends, yet in such a manner that first they be manumitted and the liberty of their state they may perceive and anew to ecclesiastical honors may they succeed. For it is irreligious to exist bound to servitude those who the dignity of the sacred order receive. Moreover whatever to such either through liberty shall have been granted or by succession shall have been owed or by any advantage shall have been conferred, it shall not be lawful for them anything from there to extraneous persons to transmit, but all things to the right of the church from which they were manumitted, it behooves after their death to pertain. To these also as to the other freedmen of the church, the approach of accusing or testifying against the church is closed. But if they shall have aspired, not only the benefit of liberty let them lack, but also the grade of honor, which not by dignity of nature but by time of necessity they merited.","De familiis ecclesiae constituere presbyteros et diaconos per parrochias liceat, quos tamen vitae rectitudo et probitas morum commendat, ea tamen ratione, ut antea manumissi libertatem status sui percipiant et denuo ad ecclesiasticos honores succedant. Irreligiosum est enim obligatos exsistere servituti, qui sacri ordinis suscipiunt dignitatem. Quicquid autem talibus aut per libertatem concessum aut successione exstiterit debitum aut quolibet commodo collatum, non licebit eis quippiam inde in extraneas personas transmittere, sed omnia ad ius ecclesiae a qua manumissi sunt, oportet post eorum obitum pertinere. His quoque sicut et ceteris ecclesiae libertis accusandi vel testificandi adversus ecclesiam aditus intercluditur. Quod si aspiraverint, non solum libertatis beneficio careant, sed etiam honoris gradu, quem non dignitate naturae, sed tempore necessitatis promeruerunt."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p80","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","LXXIV. After certain institutes of the ecclesiastical order or decrees, which pertain to the discipline of some, the final sentence for all of us priests for the strength of our kings and the stability of the Gothic nation under God the judge to bring forth is the pontifical final decree. For of many nations, as the report is, such great perfidy of minds stands out, that the faith by sacrament promised to their kings to observe they despise, and with mouth they simulate the profession of an oath, while they retain in mind the impiety of perfidy. For they swear to their kings and the faith which they promise they prevaricate, nor do they fear that volume of the judgment of God through which is brought in a curse and many threats of punishments upon those who swear in the name of God falsely. What therefore hope to such peoples against enemies laboring will there be? What faith further with other nations in peace to be trusted? What pact not to be violated? What sworn promise among enemies will remain, when not even to their own kings the sworn faith they preserve? For who is so mad that his own head with his own hand he cuts off? Those, as is known, unmindful of their own salvation with their own hand themselves slay, against themselves and their own kings their own forces turning, and while the Lord says: Touch not my anointed, and David: Who, he says, stretches out his hand against the anointed of the Lord and will be innocent? To them not to avoid is the fear of perjury nor to bring destruction upon kings. To enemies indeed the faith of a pact is given nor is violated. But if in war faith avails, how much more among their own is it to be preserved. Sacrilegious indeed it is, if be violated by nations the promised faith of their kings, because not only in them is made a transgression of the pact, but also against God indeed, in whose name that promise itself is pledged. From this it is that many kingdoms of the earth by heavenly wrath have been so changed, that on account of the impiety of faith and of morals one by another might be destroyed. Whence also us it behooves to beware of a fall of nations of this kind, lest similarly by a headlong plague we be stricken and by a cruel punishment we be punished. For if God against angels prevaricating in himself did not spare, who by disobedience the heavenly habitation lost, whence also through Isaiah he says: Drunk is my sword in heaven, how much more ought we the destruction of our own salvation to fear, lest through unfaithfulness by the same raging sword of God we perish. But if we wish to avoid divine wrath and his severity to clemency to provoke, let us preserve toward God the cultivation of religion and fear, let us guard toward our princes the promised faith and pledge. Let there not be in us as in certain nations the impious subtlety of unfaithfulness, not the perfidy of a deceitful mind, not the crime of perjury and the nefarious efforts of conspiracies. Let no one among us by presumption seize the kingdom, let no one excite mutual seditions of citizens, let no one meditate the destruction of kings, but with the prince deceased in peace let the primates of the whole nation with the priests the successor of the kingdom by common counsel establish, so that, while the concord of unity by us is retained, no rupture of the fatherland and of the nation through violence and ambition may be attempted.","Post instituta quaedam ecclesiastici ordinis vel decreta, quae ad quorundam pertinent disciplinam, postrema nobis cunctis sacerdotibus sententia est pro robore nostrorum regum et stabilitate gentis Gothorum pontificale ultimum sub deo iudice ferre decretum. Multarum quippe gentium, ut fama est, tanta exstat perfidia animorum, ut fidem sacramento promissam regibus suis observare contemnant et ore simulent iuramenti professionem, dum retineant mente perfidiae impietatem. Iurant enim regibus suis et fidem, quam pollicentur, praevaricantur nec metuunt volumen illud iudicii dei, per quod inducitur maledictio multaque poenarum comminatio super eos, qui iurant in nomine dei mendaciter. Quae igitur spes talibus populis contra hostes laborantibus erit? Quae fides ultra cum aliis gentibus in pace credenda? Quod foedus non violandum? Quae in hostibus iurata sponsio permanebit, quando nec ipsis propriis regibus iuratam fidem conservant? Quis enim adeo furiosus est, qui caput suum manu sua propria desecet? Illi, ut notum est, immemores salutis suae propria manu seipsos interimunt, in semetipsos suosque reges proprias convertendo vires, et dum dominus dicat: Nolite tangere Christos meos, et David: Quis, inquit, extendit manum suam in christum domini et innocens erit? Illis nec vitare metus est periurium nec regibus inferre exitium. Hostibus quippe fides pacti datur nec violatur. Quod si in bello fides valeat, quanto magis in suis servanda est. Sacrilegium quippe est, si violetur a gentibus regum suorum promissa fides, quia non solum in eis fit pacti transgressio, sed et in deum quidem, in cuius nomine pollicetur ipsa promissio. Inde est, quod multa regna terrarum caelestis iracundia ita permutavit, ut pro impietate fidei et morum alterum ab altero solveretur. Unde et nos cavere oportet casum huiusmodi gentium, ne similiter plaga feriamur praecipiti et poena puniamur crudeli. Si enim deus angelis in se praevaricantibus non pepercit, qui per inoboedientiam caeleste habitaculum perdiderunt, unde et per Esaiam dicit: Inebriatus est gladius meus in caelo, quanto magis nos nostrae salutis interitum timere debemus, ne per infidelitatem eodem saeviente dei gladio pereamus. Quod si divinam iracundiam vitare volumus et severitatem eius ad clementiam provocare cupimus, servemus erga deum religionis cultum atque timorem, custodiamus erga principes nostros pollicitam fidem atque sponsionem. Non sit in nobis sicut in quibusdam gentibus infidelitatis subtilitas impia, non subdolae mentis perfidia, non periurii nefas et coniurationum nefanda molimina. Nullus apud nos praesumptione regnum arripiat, nullus excitet mutuas seditiones civium, nemo meditetur interitus regum, sed defuncto in pace principe primatus totius gentis cum sacerdotibus successorem regni consilio communi constituant, ut, dum unitatis concordia a nobis retinetur, nullum patriae gentisque discidium per vim atque ambitum moliatur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p81","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","But if this admonition our minds does not correct and to the common salvation our heart by no means leads, hear our sentence: Whoever therefore from us or from the peoples of all Spain by any conspiracy or study the sacrament of his faith, which for the state of the fatherland and of the Gothic nation or for the preservation of the royal safety he has promised, shall have violated, or a king by death shall have touched, or from the power of the kingdom shall have cast out, or by tyrannical presumption the summit of the kingdom shall have usurped, let him be anathema in the sight of God the Father and of the angels, and from the catholic church, which by perjury he shall have profaned, let him be made a stranger, and from all assembly of Christians alien, and with all the associates of his impiety, because it is fitting that one penalty hold liable those whom a like error shall have found implicated. Which again a second time we repeat, saying: Whoever from now on from us or from all the peoples of Spain by any deliberation or study the sacrament of his faith, which for the state of the fatherland and of the Gothic nation or for the preservation of the royal safety he has promised, shall have violated, or a king by death shall have touched, or from the power of the kingdom shall have cast out, or by tyrannical presumption the summit of the kingdom shall have usurped, let him be anathema in the sight of Christ and his apostles, and from the catholic church, which by perjury he shall have profaned, let him be made a stranger, and from all consortia of Christians alien, and damned in the future judgment of God let him be held with his partakers, because it is worthy that those who are joined to such, they also by the participation of their damnation be held liable. This also a third time we acclaim, saying: Whoever from now on from us or from all the peoples of Spain by any meditation or study the sacrament of his faith, which for the safety of the fatherland and the state of the Gothic nation or for the safety of the royal power he has promised, shall have violated, or a king by death shall have touched, or from the power of the kingdom shall have cast out, or by tyrannical presumption the summit of the kingdom shall have usurped, let him be anathema in the sight of the Holy Spirit and of the martyrs of Christ, and from the catholic church, which by perjury he shall have profaned, let him be made a stranger, and from all communion of Christians alien, nor let him have a part with the just, but with the devil and his angels by eternal punishments let him be condemned together with those who by the same conspiracy strive, that a like punishment of perdition may bind those whom into perdition a wicked society joins. And therefore, if it pleases all who are present, let this sentence three times reiterated by the consent of your voice be strengthened.","Quod si haec admonitio mentes nostras non corrigit et ad salutem communem cor nostrum nequaquam perducit, audi sententiam nostram: Quicumque igitur a nobis vel totius Spaniae populis qualibet coniuratione vel studio sacramentum fidei suae, quod pro patriae gentisque Gothorum statu vel conservatione regiae salutis pollicitus est, temeraverit aut regem nece attrectaverit aut potestate regni exuerit aut praesumptione tyrannica regni fastigium usurpaverit, anathema sit in conspectu dei patris et angelorum atque ab ecclesia catholica, quam periurio profanaverit, efficiatur extraneus et ab omni coetu christianorum alienus et cum omnibus impietatis suae sociis, quia oportet, ut una poena teneat obnoxios, quos similis error invenerit implicatos. Quod iterum secundo replicamus dicentes: Quicumque amodo ex nobis vel cunctis Spaniae populis quolibet tractatu vel studio sacramentum fidei suae, quod pro patriae gentisque Gothorum statu vel conservatione regiae salutis pollicitus est, violaverit aut regem nece attrectaverit aut potestate regni exuerit aut praesumptione tyrannica regni fastigium usurpaverit, anathema in conspectu Christi et apostolorum eius sit atque ab ecclesia catholica, quam periurio profanaverit, efficiatur extraneus et ab omni consortio christianorum alienus et damnatus in futuro dei iudicio habeatur cum conparticibus suis, quia dignum est, ut, qui talibus sociantur, ipsi etiam damnationis eorum participatione obnoxii teneantur. Hoc etiam tertio acclamamus dicentes: Quicumque a modo ex nobis vel cunctis Spaniae populis qualibet meditatione vel studio sacramentum fidei suae, quod pro patriae salute gentisque Gothorum statu vel incolumitate regiae potestatis pollicitus est, violaverit aut regem nece tractaverit aut potestatem regni exuerit aut praesumptione tyrannica regni fastigium usurpaverit, anathema sit in conspectu spiritus sancti et martyrum Christi atque ab ecclesia catholica, quam periurio profanaverit, efficiatur extraneus et ab omni communione christianorum alienus neque partem habeat iustorum, sed cum diabolo et angelis eius aeternis suppliciis condemnetur una cum eis, qui eadem coniuratione nituntur, ut par poena perditionis constringat, quos in perniciem prava societas copulat. Et ideo, si placet omnibus, qui adestis, haec tertio reiterata sententia vestrae vocis consensu firmetur."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p82","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","By the whole clergy and people it was said: Who against this your definition shall have presumed, anathema maranatha, that is perdition in the coming of the Lord let them be, and with Judas Scariot let them have a part, both they and their associates. Amen.","Ab universo clero vel populo dictum est: Qui contra hanc vestram definitionem praesumpserint, anathema maranatha, hoc est perditio in adventu domini sint, et cum Iuda Scariot partem habeant et ipsi et socii eorum. Amen."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p83","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","On which account we ourselves all priests admonish the church of Christ and the people, that this tremenda and so often reiterated sentence no one of us in the present and eternal judgment may condemn, but the promised faith toward our most glorious lord King Sisenand guarding and with sincere devotion to him serving, not only the clemency of divine piety in us we may provoke, but even the grace of the afore-mentioned prince to receive we may merit. You also, present king, and the future of following ages princes, with the humility which we ought, we beseech, that moderate and gentle toward subjects existing with justice and piety the peoples by God to you entrusted you may rule, and good return to Christ, who you to us established as giver, you may render, reigning in humility of heart with the study of good action, nor let any one of you alone in the cause of heads or of property sentence bring, but with public consent with the rulers by manifest judgment the fault of the delinquents may be made clear, the gentleness by you in offenses being preserved, that not by severity more in them than by indulgence you may prevail, that, while all these things by God the author by pious moderation by you are preserved, both kings in the peoples and peoples in the kings and God in both may rejoice. Moreover concerning future kings this sentence we promulgate, that if anyone from them against the reverence of the laws by proud domination and royal pride in flagitious deeds and crimes or by cupidity most cruel power in the peoples shall have exercised, by the sentence of anathema by Christ the Lord let him be condemned, and let him have from God separation and judgment, on account of which he shall have presumed evil to do and into destruction the kingdom to turn.","Quapropter nos ipsi sacerdotes omnes ecclesiam Christi ac populum admonemus, ut haec tremenda et toties reiterata sententia nullum ex nobis praesenti atque aeterno condemnet iudicio, sed fidem promissam erga gloriosissimum dominum nostrum Sisenandum regem custodientes ac sincera illi devotione famulantes, non solum divinae pietatis clementiam in nobis provocemus, sed etiam gratiam antefati principis percipere mereamur. Te quoque praesentem regem futurosque aetatum sequentium principes humilitate, qua debemus, deposcimus, ut moderati et mites erga subiectos exsistentes cum iustitia et pietate populos a deo vobis creditos regatis bonamque vicissitudinem, qui vos nobis constituit largitori Christo respondeatis, regnantes in humilitate cordis cum studio bonae actionis nec quisquam vestrum solus in causam capitum aut rerum sententiam ferat, sed consensu publico cum rectoribus ex iudicio manifesto delinquentium culpa patescat servata a vobis in offensis mansuetudine, ut non severitate magis in illis quam indulgentia polleatis, ut, dum omnia haec auctore deo pio a vobis moderamine conservantur et reges in populis et populi in regibus et deus in utrisque laetetur. Sane de futuris regibus hanc sententiam promulgamus, ut, si quis ex eis contra reverentiam legum superba dominatione et fastu regio in flagitiis et facinore sive cupiditate crudelissimam potestatem in populis exercuerit, anathematis sententia a Christo domino condemnetur et habeat a deo separationem atque iudicium, propter quod praesumpserit prava agere et in perniciem regnum convertere."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p84","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","Concerning Suinthila indeed, who his own crimes fearing himself of the kingdom deprived and from the fasces of power cast off, this with the counsel of the nation we have decreed, that neither him or his wife on account of the evils which they committed, nor their sons to our unity ever we may associate, nor them to the honors from which on account of iniquity they were cast down at any time we may promote. And who even as from the summit of the kingdom are held strangers, so also from the possession of things, which from the presumption of the wretched they had consumed, let them remain strangers, besides that which by the piety of our most pious prince they shall have obtained. Not otherwise also Geila, of the remembered Suinthila both by blood and by crime brother, who neither in the faith of brotherhood stood stable nor the faith to our most glorious lord promised preserved; him therefore with his wife, just as also the afore-mentioned, from the society of the nation and from our consortium it has pleased to be separated, nor in the lost goods, in which through iniquity they had grown, to be made returns, besides that which they shall have obtained by the piety of the most clement prince, whose grace the good of lords with rewards enriches and the evil from his beneficence does not separate.","De Suintilane vero, qui scelera propria metuens seipsum regno privavit et a potestatis fascibus exuit, id cum gentis consultu decrevimus, ut neque eum vel uxorem eius propter mala, quae commiserunt, neque filios eorum unitati nostrae umquam consociemus, nec eos ad honores, a quibus ob iniquitatem deiecti sunt, aliquando promoveamus, quique etiam sicut fastigio regni habentur extranei, ita et possessione rerum, quas de miserorum praesumptibus hauserant, maneant alieni praeter id, quod pietate piissimi principis nostri fuerint consecuti. Non aliter et Geilanem memorati Suintilani et sanguine et scelere fratrem, qui nec in germanitatis fide stabilis exstitit nec fidem gloriosissimo domino nostro pollicitam conservavit, hunc igitur cum coniuge sua, sicut et antefatum a societate gentis atque consortio nostro placuit separari, nec in amissis facultatibus, in quibus per iniquitatem creverant, reduces fieri praeter id, quod consecuti fuerint pietate clementissimi principis, cuius gratia bonos dominorum praemiis ditat et malos a beneficentia sua non separat."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p85","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","Glory and honor to our almighty God, in whose name we are gathered. After these things, peace, salvation, and long life to the most pious and lover of Christ our lord King Sisenand, whose devotion us to this salutary decree convoked. May the glory of Christ strengthen his kingdom and that of the Gothic nation in the catholic faith; with years and merits may the grace of the most high God protect him unto extreme old age, and after the glory of the present kingdom may he pass to the eternal kingdom, that without end he may reign who in the world happily rules, he himself granting who is King of kings and Lord of lords, with the Father and with the Holy Spirit unto ages of ages. Amen.","Gloria autem et honor omnipotenti deo nostro, in cuius nomine congregati sumus. Post haec pax, salus et diuturnitas piissimo et amatori Christi domino nostro Sisenando regi, cuius devotio nos ad hoc decretum salutiferum convocavit. Corroboret Christi gloria regnum illius gentisque Gothorum in fide catholica, annis et meritis protegat illum usque ad ultimam senectutem summi dei gratia et post praesentis regni gloriam ad aeternum regnum transeat, ut sine fine regnet, qui in saeculo feliciter imperat ipso praestante, qui est rex regum et dominus dominorum cum patre et cum spiritu sancto in saecula saeculorum. Amen."],["vol3/toledo-iv/index.html","p86","Concerning the Conversation of Clerics","These things therefore having been defined, which above are comprehended, the most religious prince assenting, it pleased thereafter nothing impeding by any one of us those things which have been established to be violated, but all things by salutary counsel to preserve, and because to the profits of the church and of our soul they agree, also by our own subscription, that they may remain, we strengthen. I, Isidore, in the name of Christ metropolitan bishop of the church of Seville, establishing these things, have subscribed. Similarly also the other bishops subscribed, in number 40.","Definitis itaque his, quae superius compraehensa sunt, annuente religiosissimo principe, placuit deinde nulla re impediente a quolibet nostrum ea, quae constituta sunt, temerari, sed cuncta salubri consilio conservare et, quia profectibus ecclesiae et animae nostrae conveniunt, etiam propria subscriptione, ut permaneant, roboramus. Ego Isidorus in Christi nomine ecclesiae Spalensis metropolitanus episcopus haec statuens subscripsi. Similiter et alii episcopi subscripserunt numero XL."]];